 \label{d:frozen.model}
Throughout this paper we take the convention that if $\SPIN{L}\in\set{\SPIN{-},\SPIN{+}}$ and $x=\SPIN{f}$, then 
$\SPIN{L} x\equiv\SPIN{f}$.
On a $\textup{$k$-\textsc{sat}}$ instance $\mathscr{G}=(V,F,E)$, a \bemph{frozen configuration} is a vector $\smash{\vec{x}}\in\set{\SPIN{+},\SPIN{-},\SPIN{f}}^V$ such that
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item \label{i:frozen.sat.clause} Each clause $a\in F$ is \bemph{satisfied}, meaning
that for at least one $e\in\delta a$ we have $\SPIN{L}_e x_{v(e)} \in \set{\SPIN{+},\SPIN{f}}$;
\item \label{i:frozen.var.forced}
A variable $v\in V$ takes value $x_v\ne\SPIN{f}$
if and only if it is \bemph{forced} to do so,
meaning that for at least one $e\in\delta v$ we have
$\SPIN{L}_{e'}x_{v(e')} = \SPIN{-} \SPIN{L}_e x_{v(e)}$ for all $e'\in\delta a(e)\setminus e$.
\end{enumerate}
(The definition makes sense even in the presence of multi-edges.)

\label{r:coarsen}
Place a graph structure on the set 
$\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize SOL}}(\mathscr{G})\equiv\mathscr{G}^{-1}(1)$ of satisfying assignments by putting an edge between any pair of assignments at Hamming distance one, and define a \bemph{cluster} of solutions to be a (maximal) connected component of the graph $\mathscr{G}^{-1}(1)$. As has been explained in the literature (see e.g.\ \cite{parisi_whitening,MR2351840,MR2518205}), frozen configurations encode clusters in the sense that there is a natural mapping
	\begin{equation}\label{e:coarsen}
	\textup{\textsf{coarsen}}:
	\mathscr{G}^{-1}(1)
	\longrightarrow
	\bigg\{
	\textup{frozen configurations on $\mathscr{G}$}
	\bigg\}\,.
	\end{equation}
If $\mathscr{G}$ has no multi-edges, then each cluster $\mathscr{C}\subseteq \mathscr{G}^{-1}(1)$ maps to a single frozen configuration $\textup{\textsf{coarsen}}(\mathscr{C})$. To define the map, given a configuration $\smash{\vec{x}}\in\set{\SPIN{+},\SPIN{-},\SPIN{f}}^V$
that does not violate any clauses
(in the sense of Definition~\ref{d:frozen.model}\eqref{i:frozen.sat.clause}),
 let us say that a variable $v\in V$ is \bemph{blocked} with respect to $\smash{\vec{x}}$ if there is some $e\in\delta v$ such that $\SPIN{L}_{e'}x_{v(e')} = \SPIN{-} \SPIN{L}_e x_{v(e)}$ for all $e'\in\delta a(e)\setminus e$ (cf.\ Definition~\ref{d:frozen.model}\eqref{i:frozen.var.forced}). Define $\textup{\textsf{co}}(\smash{\vec{x}}) = \vec{y}$ where
 	\[
	y_v=\left\{\hspace{-3pt}
	\begin{array}{cl}
	x_v & \textup{if $v$ is blocked with respect to $\smash{\vec{x}}$;}\\
	\SPIN{f} & \textup{otherwise.}
	\end{array}
	\right.
	\]
The map of \eqref{e:coarsen} is defined by iterating $\textup{\textsf{co}}$ until the configuration stabilizes, i.e., $\textup{\textsf{coarsen}}\equiv\textup{\textsf{co}}^\infty$. Note that termination happens in finite time, since if $\textup{\textsf{co}}(\smash{\vec{x}})\ne\smash{\vec{x}}$ then $\textup{\textsf{co}}(\smash{\vec{x}})$ has strictly more $\SPIN{f}$ variables than $\smash{\vec{x}}$. If $\mathscr{G}$ has no multi-edges, then the value of $\textup{\textsf{co}}(\smash{\vec{x}})$ at any variable $v$ is a function of the neighboring values $x_u$, $u\in N(v)$. Consequently, if $\smash{\vec{x}}$ and $\smash{\vec{x}}'$ differ in a single coordinate, then $\textup{\textsf{co}}(\smash{\vec{x}})=\textup{\textsf{co}}(\smash{\vec{x}}')$. Iterating this gives $\textup{\textsf{coarsen}}(\smash{\vec{x}})=\textup{\textsf{coarsen}}(\smash{\vec{x}}')$ as long as $\smash{\vec{x}},\smash{\vec{x}}'$ lie in the same cluster. This shows that the map \eqref{e:coarsen} takes each cluster $\mathscr{C}$ to a single frozen configuration.

\[
	y_v=\left\{\hspace{-3pt}
	\begin{array}{cl}
	x_v & \textup{if $v$ is blocked with respect to $\smash{\vec{x}}$;}\\
	\SPIN{f} & \textup{otherwise.}
	\end{array}
	\right.
	\]

\label{r:cube}
Another mapping one might consider is
$\textup{\textsf{cube}}: \mathscr{G}^{-1}(1) \to \set{\SPIN{+},\SPIN{-},\SPIN{f}}^V$,
where $\textup{\textsf{cube}}(\smash{\vec{x}})=\vec{y}$ is defined by
	\[y_v=\left\{\hspace{-3pt}
	\begin{array}{cl}
	x_v & \textup{if $v$ only takes value $x_v$
		in the cluster containing $\smash{\vec{x}}$;}\\
	\SPIN{f} & \textup{otherwise.}
	\end{array}\right.\]
This also has the property that each cluster is mapped to a single point in $\set{\SPIN{+},\SPIN{-},\SPIN{f}}^V$, which in this case simply encodes the minimal Hamming subcube containing that cluster. In many situations the maps $\textup{\textsf{coarsen}}$ and $\textup{\textsf{cube}}$ are identical, but one can construct cases where they differ; see Figure~\ref{f:coarsening.example}. The frozen model is preferable precisely because it is defined only by \bemph{local} constraints.

\[y_v=\left\{\hspace{-3pt}
	\begin{array}{cl}
	x_v & \textup{if $v$ only takes value $x_v$
		in the cluster containing $\smash{\vec{x}}$;}\\
	\SPIN{f} & \textup{otherwise.}
	\end{array}\right.\]

