For a clause $a\in F$, if the tuple $\pi\equiv(\pi_e)_{e\in\delta a}$ is strictly coherent and further satisfies $\pi_e(\SSPIN{g})>0$ for all $e\in\delta a$, then there exists a set of edge weights $w_e : \set{\SSPIN{r},\SSPIN{y},\SSPIN{g},\SSPIN{b}} \to [0,\infty)$ such that the $w$-weighted measure on valid colorings of $\delta a$ has edge marginals consistent with $\pi$. Moreover, if we fix $w_e(\SSPIN{g})\equiv1$ for all $e\in\delta a$ and require that $\textup{supp} w_e \subseteq \textup{supp}\pi_e$, then $w$ is unique.


\begin{proof} We will obtain the weights under the assumption that $w_e(\SSPIN{g})\equiv1$, and $w_e(\sigma)=0$ if $\sigma\notin\textup{supp}{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi_e$; this will imply the result. We can thus restrict our attention to $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize PR}}_{\delta a}$, the space of all probability measures over $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$. In the case that $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$ consists of a single element, that element must be the all-$\SSPIN{g}$ coloring. Then $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize PR}}_{\delta a}$ also consists of a single element, which is the probability measure fully supported on the all-$\SSPIN{g}$ coloring, and setting $w_e(\sigma)\equiv\Ind{\sigma=\SSPIN{g}}$ gives the unique weights satisfying the stated conditions.

We therefore assume for the remainder of the proof that $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$ has at least two elements. 
Recall that $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}$ denotes the space of all edge marginals $\pi\equiv (\pi_e)_{e\in\delta a}$ which can arise from probability measures over $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$. We now claim that the space $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}$ has dimension
	\[
	\dim\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}
	= D_a =
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	\bigg\{|\textup{supp}{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi_e|-1\bigg\}
	\,.\]
Indeed, it is clear that $\dim\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a} \le D_a$. Equality follows from the strict coherence assumption: let $\acute{\pi}$ be any tuple of measures $(\acute{\pi}_e)_{e\in\delta a}$, subject \emph{only} to the constraints that $\acute{\pi}_e$ and $\pi_e$ have the same support for all $e\in\delta a$, and moreover that $\|\pi-\acute{\pi}\|_\infty\le\delta$. The set of such perturbations $\acute{\pi}$ has dimension $D_a$. Since $\pi$ is strictly coherent, we have that $\acute{\pi}$ is also coherent for small enough $\delta$. It then follows from Lemma~\ref{l:coher.feasibility} that there is a probability measure $\acute{\nu}$ on $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$ with marginal $\acute{\pi}$, which means that $\acute{\pi}\in\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}$. Since $\acute{\pi}$ goes over a $D_a$-dimensional space, this proves $\dim\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}=D_a$. 


Let $A$ be the linear mapping that takes $\nu\in\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize PR}}_{\delta a}$ to its edge marginals $\pi_e(\sigma)$, for $e\in\delta a$ and $\sigma\in\textup{supp}\pi_e\setminus\set{\SSPIN{g}}$. We can regard $A$ as a matrix with row indices $\set{(e,\sigma') : e\in\delta a, \sigma'\in\textup{supp}\pi_e\setminus\set{\SSPIN{g}}}$, and column indices $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$. The entry of $A$ with row index $(e,\sigma')$ and column index $\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}$ is the indicator that $\sigma_e = \sigma'$. The image of $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize PR}}_{\delta a}$ under $A$ is in one-to-one correspondence with $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}$, whose dimension $D_a$ exactly equals the number of rows of $A$. This shows that $A$ is of full rank. Moreover, let $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MSR}}_{\delta a}$ be the space of all nonnegative \emph{measures} (not necessarily normalized to unit mass) over $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$. The image of $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MSR}}_{\delta a}$ under the matrix
	\begin{equation}\label{e:matrix.A.prime.clause}
	A'\equiv
	\begin{pmatrix}
	A \\ \mathbf{1}^\textup{\textsf{t}}
	\end{pmatrix}\end{equation}
is the space of all nonnegative scalar multiples of elements of $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize MARG}}_{\delta a}$, and has dimension $D_a+1$. It follows that the matrix $A'$ is also of full rank.

Now consider the constrained entropy maximization problem
	\begin{equation}\label{e:primal}
	\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize OPT}}\equiv\max\bigg\{
	-\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}\in \textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}
	\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\ln
	\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\,\bigg|\, 
	\nu \in [0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}
	\textup{ with }
	A\nu = \pi
	\textup{ and }
	\langle \mathbf{1},\nu\rangle = 1
	\bigg\}\,,\end{equation}
as well as its Lagrange dual: for $(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$
where $\mathbf{s}\equiv(s_{e,\sigma'}:e\in\delta a, \sigma'\in\textup{supp}\pi_e\setminus\set{\SSPIN{g}})\in\mathbb{R}^{D_a}$ and $\bar{s}\in\mathbb{R}$, 
	\begin{equation}\label{e:lagrange.dual}
	g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})
	= \max\bigg\{
	-\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}\in \textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}
	\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\ln
	\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})+
	\langle \mathbf{s},
		A\nu-\pi
		\rangle
	+ \bar{s}( \langle\mathbf{1},\nu\rangle -1)
	\,\bigg|\, 
	\nu \in [0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}\bigg\}\,.\end{equation}
By the strict coherence condition together with Lemma~\ref{l:clause.strict.coherence}, there exists $\bar{\nu}_{\delta a} \in (0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}$ satisfying $A\bar{\nu}_{\delta a} = \pi$. This implies that the constraints of the (primal) optimization problem
\eqref{e:primal} are feasible, so $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize OPT}}$ is well-defined. Since the domain $(0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}$ is an open set, it follows by a classical result (see e.g.\ \cite[Thm.~28.2]{MR1451876}) that this problem enjoys strong Lagrange duality. This means that there exists $(\mathbf{S},\bar{S})$ (\emph{a~priori}, not necessarily unique) such that
	\begin{equation}\label{e:lagrange.dual.problem}
	\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize OPT}}= g(\mathbf{S},\bar{S})
	= \min_{\mathbf{s},\bar{s}}
	\bigg\{
	g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})
	: \mathbf{s} \in\mathbb{R}^{D_a},
		\bar{s} \in \mathbb{R}
	\bigg\}\,,\end{equation}
that is to say, the Lagrange dual problem (the right-hand side of \eqref{e:lagrange.dual.problem}) achieves the same value as the constrained (primal) problem \eqref{e:primal}. Now, for any fixed $(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$, the value \eqref{e:lagrange.dual} of $g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$ is given by optimizing a strictly concave objective over all $\nu\in[0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}$, so it is attained by a unique maximizer $\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]$. Likewise, in the primal problem \eqref{e:primal}, since the entropy is strictly concave, the value of $\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize OPT}}$ must be uniquely attained by some measure $\nu^\textup{op}$. In the definition~\ref{e:lagrange.dual} of $g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$, by substituting $\nu^\textup{op}$ into the objective, we see that $g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})\ge \textup{\textsf{\footnotesize OPT}}$. If equality holds, then 
$\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]$ must equal 
$\nu^\textup{op}$. This shows that
$\nu[\mathbf{S},\bar{S}]$ solves \eqref{e:primal}.


We next argue that the pair $(\mathbf{S},\bar{S})$ is unique. To this end, note that for any $(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$, the maximizer $\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]$ cannot occur on the boundary of $\nu\in[0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}$, because
	\[
	\lim_{\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})\downarrow0}
	\frac{-\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\ln\nu (\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})}
		{\nu(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})} = \infty\,.
	\]
Therefore $\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]$ is the unique stationary point of the objective in $(0,\infty)^{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}$, and we can solve for it by differentiation with respect to $\nu$. It gives, for all $\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}\in\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}$,
	\[
	\Big(\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]\Big)(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	= \exp\bigg\{
	(A^\textup{\textsf{t}}\mathbf{s})_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}}
	+\bar{s}-1\bigg\}
	= \frac{\exp(\bar{s})}{e}
	\prod_{e\in\delta a} \exp(s_{e,\sigma_e})\,,
	\]
where for the final expression to make sense we define $s_{e,\SSPIN{g}}=0$. The corresponding objective value is
	\[
	g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})
	=\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}\in\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}
		\exp\bigg\{
	(A^\textup{\textsf{t}}\mathbf{s})_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}}
		+\bar{s}-1\bigg\}
	 -\langle \mathbf{s},\pi\rangle-\bar{s}
	=\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}\in\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}
	\exp\bigg\{
	(A')^\textup{\textsf{t}} \begin{pmatrix}
		\mathbf{s} \\ \bar{s}
		\end{pmatrix}-1
	\bigg\}
	-\langle \mathbf{s},\pi\rangle-\bar{s} \,,
	\]
which is clearly strictly convex as a function of 
$(A')^\textup{\textsf{t}}(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$. Since $(A')^\textup{\textsf{t}}$
is a $|\set{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}|\times (D_a+1)$
matrix of rank $D_a+1$, it defines an injective mapping,
so we conclude that $g$ is also strictly convex as a function of the Lagrange variables $(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})$.
 This shows that the Lagrange dual problem
\eqref{e:lagrange.dual.problem} has a unique minimizer $(\mathbf{S},\bar{S})$.

In summary, we have shown that
$\nu[\mathbf{S},\bar{S}]=\nu^\textup{op}$, the unique solution of the primal problem \eqref{e:primal}. Moreover, for any $(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})\ne (\mathbf{S},\bar{S})$ the measure
$\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]$ does not satisfy the constraints of \eqref{e:primal}: supposing that it did, the fact that $(\mathbf{S},\bar{S})$ is the unique minimizer of $g$ would give
	\[
	g(\mathbf{S},\bar{S})
	<g(\mathbf{s},\bar{s})
	= -\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a}\in \textup{\textsf{\footnotesize COLS}}_{\delta a}}
	\Bigg\{
	\Big(\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]\Big)(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\Bigg\}
	\ln\Bigg\{
	\Big(\nu[\mathbf{s},\bar{s}]\Big)(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\Bigg\}
	\,,
	\]
which gives a contradiction since $\nu[\mathbf{S},\bar{S}]=\nu^\textup{op}$. The claimed result follows by setting $w_e(\sigma)=\exp(s_{e,\sigma})$ for all $e\in\delta a$, $\sigma\in\textup{supp}\pi_e$.
\end{proof}

