there must exist a measure $\mu_{\bm{T}}$ on $\set{\SPIN{-},\SPIN{+},\SPIN{f}}^2$ which is consistent with $\pi_{\bm{t}}$ for every $\bm{t}\in\bm{T}$. The measure $\mu_{\bm{T}}$ must also be judicious, in the sense that its single-copy marginals are consistent with ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi_{\bm{t}}$ for $\bm{t}\in\bm{T}$. Recall that $x_{\bm{T}}\in\set{0,1,2}$ counts the number of frozen spins that can appear at a variable of type $\bm{T}$. Let
	\[
	C' = \begin{pmatrix} C \\ \mathbf{1}^\textup{\textsf{t}}
	\end{pmatrix}
	\in\mathbb{R}^{(1+x_{\bm{T}})\times(1+x_{\bm{T}})}\,,
	\]
where for each frozen spin that can appear at $\bm{T}$, the matrix $C$ contains a row which is the indicator of that spin. Then the matrix
	\[
	C''
	= \begin{pmatrix} 
	C\otimes \mathbf{1}^\textup{\textsf{t}}\\
	\mathbf{1}^\textup{\textsf{t}}\otimes C\\
	\mathbf{1}^\textup{\textsf{t}}\otimes \mathbf{1}^\textup{\textsf{t}}
	\end{pmatrix}
	\in\mathbb{R}^{
	(2x_{\bm{T}}+1)
	\times (1+x_{\bm{T}})^2}
	\]
encodes the judicious constraints on $\mu_{\bm{T}}$ in the pair model. It follows that
	\[
	d_2(\mu_{\bm{T}})
	\equiv\dim\bigg\{
	\mu_{\bm{T}}
	:\textup{$\mu_{\bm{T}}$ is judicious}\bigg\}
	=\dim\ker C''
	=(1+x_{\bm{T}})^2
	-\bigg( 2x_{\bm{T}}+1\bigg)
	=(x_{\bm{T}})^2\,.
	\]
Now note that
for each $\bm{t}\in\bm{T}$, the choice of $\mu_{\bm{T}}$ already fixes $\pi_{\bm{t}}$ as a measure on $\set{\SSPIN{p},\SSPIN{y},\SSPIN{g}}^2$. It remains to separate \SPIN{purple} into \SPIN{red} and \SPIN{blue} in such a way that the resulting $\pi_{\bm{t}}$ has single-copy marginals ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi_{\bm{t}}$:
	\begin{align*}
	d_2(\pi_{\bm{t}}\,|\,\mu_{\bm{T}})
	&\equiv\dim\bigg\{
	\pi_{\bm{t}}
	:\textup{$\pi_{\bm{t}}$ is judicious
	and consistent with $\mu_{\bm{T}}$}
	\bigg\}\\
	&=
	\dim\bigg\{
	\pi_{\bm{t}}
	:\textup{$\pi_{\bm{t}}$ is judicious}
	\bigg\}
	-\dim\bigg\{
	\mu_{\bm{T}}
	:\textup{$\mu_{\bm{T}}$ is judicious}\bigg\}
	=( s_{\bm{t}}-1)^2
	-(x_{\bm{T}})^2\,.
	\end{align*}
In order for $\omega$ to be consistent with $\pi_{\bm{t}}$, it must satisfy the linear constraints 
\eqref{e:pi.as.avg.of.omega.for.dim}. Note that some of these are redundant with the judicious constraints on the individual measures $\omega_{\bm{L},j}$. Altogether
	\begin{align*}
	d_2( (\omega_{\bm{L},j})_{\bm{L}(j)=\bm{t}} \,|\,\pi_{\bm{t}})
	&\equiv\dim\bigg\{
	(\omega_{\bm{L},j})_{\bm{L}(j)=\bm{t}}:
	\textup{$\omega$ is 
	judicious and consistent with $\pi_{\bm{t}}$}\bigg\}\\
	&=\sum_{\bm{L},j : \bm{L}(j)=t}
	( s_{\bm{L}(j)}-1)^2
	- (s_{\bm{t}}-1)^2
	\end{align*}
