Recall from Definition~\ref{d:good.exc} that an acyclic variable $v$ is termed $\mathfrak{i}$-excellent if its neighborhood $T=B_{10R'}(v)$ satisfies condition~\eqref{e:j.excellent}, which we repeat here for convenience:
	\[
	p_{\textup{ex},\mathfrak{i}}(T)\equiv
	{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}\left( 
	\left.\begin{array}{c}
	B_{20R'}(u)
	\text{ contains 
	any}\\
	\text{variable which is not $\mathfrak{i}$-fair}
	\end{array}
	\, \right| \, B_{10R'}(u) \cong T
	\right) \le \frac1{\exp\{k^3 R' \}}\,.
	\]
By Markov's inequality and iterated expectations,
	\begin{align*}
	p
	&\equiv
	{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}\Big(\text{$v_\textup{\textsc{rt}}$ not $\mathfrak{i}$-excellent}\Big)
	\le
	\int
	\frac{p_{\textup{ex},\mathfrak{i}}(
		B_{10R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T}) )}
		{1/\exp\{k^3 R' \}}
	\,d{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}(\mathscr{T})\\
	&=\exp\{k^3 R' \}
	\,{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}\bigg(
	\textup{$B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})$ contains any variable which is not $\mathfrak{i}$-fair}
	\bigg)\,.
	\end{align*}
By another application of Markov's inequality,
together with the unimodularity property
\eqref{e:unimodular}, we find
	\begin{align*}
	p &\le\exp\{k^3 R' \}
	\int
	\sum_{u\in B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})}
	\mathbf{1}\bigg\{\textup{$u$ is not $\mathfrak{i}$-fair}\bigg\}
	\,d{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}(\mathscr{T})\\
	&=\exp\{k^3 R' \}
	\int
	|B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})|
	\mathbf{1}
	\Big\{\textup{$v_\textup{\textsc{rt}}$ not $\mathfrak{i}$-fair}\Big\}
	\,d{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}(\mathscr{T})
	\le
	p_\textup{small} + p_\textup{large}
	\,,
	\end{align*}
where $p_\textup{small}$ is the contribution from
the event
$|B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})|
	\le \exp\{k^2R\}$,
	and $p_\textup{large}$
is the contribution from
the complementary event. It follows from Corollary~\ref{c:fair} that
	\[
	p_\textup{small}
	=\exp\{ k^3 R'+ k^2 R\}
	{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}\Big(\textup{$v_\textup{\textsc{rt}}$ not $\mathfrak{i}$-fair}\Big)
	\le \frac1{\exp( 2^{k\delta_*/3}R/k^2) }\,.
	\]
The contribution from the complementary event is
	\begin{align*}
	\frac{p_\textup{large}}{\exp\{k^3 R'\}}
	&=
	\int 
	|B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})|
	\mathbf{1}
	\bigg\{
	|B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})|
	\ge \exp\{k^2R\}
	\bigg\}\,d{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}(\mathscr{T})\\
	&\le
	\exp\{k^2R\}
	{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}\bigg(|B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})|
	\ge \exp\{k^2R\}\bigg)
	+\int_{\exp\{k^2R\}}^\infty
		{\textup{\textbf{PGW}}}\bigg(|B_{20R'}(v_\textup{\textsc{rt}};\mathscr{T})|
	\ge s\bigg)\,ds\,.
	\end{align*}
It follows from Lemma~\ref{l:martingale.bound.PGW} that this is very small: $p_\textup{large} \le 1/\exp(\exp(kR))$, which is negligible compared with the bound on $p_\textup{small}$. Combining these gives the claimed bound (it is easy to show, using Markov's inequality, that the probability for $v_\textup{\textsc{rt}}$ to be improper is negligible).

