\label{c:marked.blocks.corrupt.tree} Suppose $\mathscr{G}=(V,F,E)$ is a finite bipartite factor graph in which all clauses have degree $k$, and which can be expressed as \[\mathscr{G}=\mathscr{T}\cup\mathscr{G}'\] where $\mathscr{G}'$ is an arbitrary graph, and $\mathscr{T}$ is a tree that intersects $\mathscr{G}'$ at a single variable $v$, such that $\mathscr{T}$ has depth $L$ when rooted at $v$. If $A$ is a subset of $V$ such that $\textup{\textsf{BSP}}'(A;\mathscr{G})=\mathscr{G}$, then there is a $1/(80R)$-corrupted subtree $T\subseteq\mathscr{T}$ of maximum degree at most four, with $L \le \textup{diam} T \le \textup{diam} \mathscr{T} \le 2L$.

\begin{proof}
The proof of Lemma~\ref{l:marked.blocks.corrupt.tree} applies to $\mathscr{T}$; the only difference is that we fix $v$ to be the root of $\mathscr{T}$. In the final step of extracting $B$ from $B'$, it is easy to arrange that none of the variables in $B$ lie in the topmost block of $\mathscr{T}$. By construction, the tree $T$ has depth exactly $L$, hence diameter between $L$ and $2L$. Then the requirement that $T\cup B_R(B;\mathscr{G})$ be acyclic is satisfied, since this will be a subgraph of the tree $\mathscr{T}$.
\end{proof}

