In words, we interpret the above decomposition as follows. To sample $\mathscr{G}\sim\mathbb{P}_{n,m}$, we start with $n$ variables and $m$ clauses where each clause is equipped with $k$ outgoing edges (indexed $j=1,\ldots,k$). Each outgoing edge matches to a uniformly random variable, independently of all other edges --- this means that we can sample $\mathscr{G}$ in a \emph{sequential} way, revealing one edge at a time. In particular, when we fix a pair $(\zeta',\bm{j})$ and condition on the event $\set{\zeta' : T' \hookrightarrow_{\bm{j}} \mathscr{G}}$, it is equivalent to say that for all $a\in F_{T'}$ and all $e=(av)\in\delta a$, we reveal that the $\bm{j}(e)$-th edge incident to clause $\zeta'(a)$ matches to the variable $\zeta'(v)$. Thus
	\[\mathbb{P}_{n,m}\bigg(\zeta' : T' \hookrightarrow_{\bm{j}} \mathscr{G}\bigg)
	= \frac1{n^{|E_{T'}|}}\,,\]
and conditioning on the event $\set{\zeta' : T' \hookrightarrow_{\bm{j}} \mathscr{G}}$ reveals nothing about the remaining $mk-|E_{T'}|$ edges in the graph $\mathscr{G}$. 
Note also that the total number of pairs $(\zeta',\bm{j}')$ is upper bounded by $\smash{n^{|V_{T'}|-1}m^{|F_{T'}|}k^{|E_{T'}|}}$.

Now fix $(\zeta',\bm{j})$ and condition on the event $\set{\zeta' : T' \hookrightarrow_{\bm{j}} \mathscr{G}}$. We next want to explore the $R$-neighborhoods of the variables in $\zeta'(B')$. Note that this can also be done sequentially, in a ``breadth-first search'' manner:
first take any $u\in \zeta'(B')$, and reveal all the edges incident to it.
