\label{l:lbd.Ea}
Let $\mathbb{P}=\mathbb{P}_{n,m}$ for $|m-n\alpha| \le n^{1/2}\ln n$, and let $\mathscr{G}$ denote a sample from $\mathbb{P}$. There is a positive constant $c_0(k,R)$ such that for all $n$ large enough, we have
	\[
	\mathbb{P}\Big( \mathscr{G}\in \bm{E}_a(\bm{L})\Big) \ge c_0(k,R)\,.
	\]
for all $a\in[m]$ and all clause total types $\bm{L}$ that are feasible in the sense of Definition~\ref{d:feasible.clause.type}.

\begin{proof} We first argue that there is a finite constant $C(k,R)$ such that
	\begin{equation}\label{e:frak.L.bounded}
	\bigg|\bigg\{
	\hspace{-3pt}\begin{array}{c}
	\textup{feasible clause total} \\ 
	\textup{types $\bm{L}\equiv(L_0,L_\infty)$}
	\end{array}\hspace{-3pt}
	\bigg\}\bigg| \le C_0(k,R)\,.
	\end{equation}
To this end, let $\bm{L}\equiv(L_0,L_\infty)$ be a feasible type, with $\mathscr{H},a_\star$ as in Definition~\ref{d:feasible.clause.type}. Recall that the initial type $L_0$ encodes the $(R+1/2)$-neighborhood of $a_\star$ in $\mathscr{H}$. If any variable in the $(R-1/2)$-neighborhood of $a_\star$ in $\mathscr{H}$ has degree more than $\exp\{k^2 (5R')\}$, then one of the variables $u\in a_\star$ will fail to be $1$-fair in $\mathscr{H}$ (since it will violate property \eqref{i:fair.volume.bound} in Definition~\ref{d:perfect.fair}). But then the $R$-neighborhood of $u$ will be removed in the initial stage of processing on $\mathscr{H}$, contradicting the assumption that $a_\star\in\textup{\textsf{pr}}\mathscr{H}$. This proves that all variables in $L_0$ must have degree at most
$\exp\{k^2 (5R')\}$. All variables in the final type $L_\infty$ must be $1$-good, hence also $1$-fair, so they must also have degree at most
$\exp\{k^2 (5R')\}$. Finally $L_0$ is a tree of depth at most $R$, while $L_\infty$ is a tree of depth at most $R(1+1/100)$. This proves \eqref{e:frak.L.bounded}. Since we fix a mapping from $\bm{L}$ to $\mathscr{T}(\bm{L})$ (see Definition~\ref{d:tree.based.on.feasible.type}), it immediately follows from \eqref{e:frak.L.bounded} that the number of distinct $\mathscr{T}(\bm{L})$ is also upper bounded by $C_0(k,R)$.

Now take $\mathbb{P}=\mathbb{P}_{n,m}$ as in the statement of the lemma, fix $a\in[m]$, and let $\mathscr{G}\sim\mathbb{P}$. By revealing the neighborhood of of $a$ in $\mathscr{G}$ in breadth-first fashion, it is easy to see that $\mathbb{P}(\bm{E}_a(\bm{L}) )$ is lower bounded by a constant which depends only on $\mathscr{T}(\bm{L})$. It then follows from the above that in fact there is a constant $c_0(k,R)$ such that (for $n$ large enough) we have $\mathbb{P}(\bm{E}_a(\bm{L}) ) \ge c_0(k,R)$ for all feasible $\bm{L}$. This proves the lemma.
\end{proof}

