\label{d:expansion}
In the processed graph $\mathscr{G}=(V,F,E)$, 
for any subset of variables $S\subseteq V$, let $ F_\bullet(S)$ denote the subset of clauses
$a\in F$ with $|S\cap \partial a|\ge 9k/10$.
We say that a subset of variables $S\subseteq V$, is a \bemph{type-subset}
if membership in $S$ can be determined by the variable type alone --- i.e., for all $v\in S$ and $w\notin S$,
we have $\bm{T}_v \ne \bm{T}_w$.
We say that $\mathscr{G}$ \bemph{expands on type-subsets} if every type-subset $S\subseteq V$ satisfies the bound
	\begin{equation}\label{e:expansion.bd}
	|F_\bullet(S)|\le \begin{cases}
		|V| 2^{29k/30} & 
		\textup{if $|S|/|V| \le 4/5$,}\\
		|S| &\textup{if $|S|/|V| \le 1/16$}
		\end{cases}\end{equation}
Whether $\mathscr{G}$ expands on type-subsets
can be determined from the neighborhood profile $\mathscr{D}$, so we say equivalently that $\mathscr{D}$ \bemph{expands on type-subsets}.

\label{p:apriori}
Let $\mathscr{G}'$ be the random $\textup{$k$-\textsc{sat}}$ instance, and $\mathscr{G}=\textup{\textsf{pr}}\mathscr{G}'$ the processed graph, with neighborhood profile $\mathscr{D}$. Recall Lemma~\ref{l:if.neg.def}, and let
	\begin{equation}\label{e:opt.omega.of.DD}
	\omega(\mathscr{D})
	\in
	\textup{arg\,max}_\omega
	\Bigg\{
	\bm{\Psi}_{\mathscr{D},2}(\omega)
	:\omega\in\bm{I}_0
	\Bigg\}\,.
	\end{equation}
(We do not yet argue that $\omega(\mathscr{D})$ is unique;
that will be shown in the \hyperlink{p:second.moment.judicious.proof}{proof of 
Proposition~\ref{p:second.moment.judicious}}.) There exists an absolute constant $\zeta_*>0$ such that if $\mathscr{D}$ expands on type-subsets
in the sense of Definition~\ref{d:expansion}, then
any maximizer $\omega(\mathscr{D})$ must satisfy the \textup{``a~priori''} bounds
	\begin{equation}\label{e:apriori}
	\Bigg|
		\frac{\omega_{\bm{L},j}(\sigma)}
		{{}_{*}\omega_{\bm{L},j}(\sigma)}
		- 1 \Bigg| \le
		\begin{cases}
		2^{-k\zeta_*}& \textup{if $\SSPIN{r}[\sigma]\le1$,}\\
		2^{k(1-\zeta_*)}&\textup{if $\SSPIN{r}[\sigma]=2$.}
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
whenever $\bm{L}(j)$ is a strongly non-defective edge type in the sense of Remark~\ref{r:defective.clauses}.

\label{r:relative.entropy}
We now informally describe the two main ways that we will apply the identity obtained in Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy}.
Let $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$ be an affine subspace of the simplex of probability measures over $\mathcal{A}$.

\begin{enumerate}[A.]
\item Suppose that $\nu(a)=\Lambda(a)/z$ where $\ln\Lambda$ are the Lagrange multipliers for the subspace $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$, such that $\nu$ is the solution of the optimization problem 
	\begin{equation}\label{e:constrained.opt.abstract}
	\textup{arg\,max}_{\nu'}
	\Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\nu')
	: \nu' \in \textup{\textsf{LIN}}\Bigg\}\,.\end{equation}
If $\mu$ is any element of $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$, then Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy} gives
	\begin{equation}\label{e:relative.entropy.with.Lagrangian}
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	=\Bigg\{
	\underbrace{\mathcal{H}(\nu)- \mathcal{H}(\mu)}
		_{\textup{nonnegative}}\Bigg\}
	+\underbrace{
		\Big\langle \nu-\mu,\ln\Lambda\Big\rangle
		}_{\textup{zero}}
	=\mathcal{H}(\nu)- \mathcal{H}(\mu)\,,
	\end{equation}
where the last equality uses that 
$\langle\mu,\ln\Lambda\rangle$ must be constant over $\mu\in\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$, by the nature of Lagrange multipliers.
In the \hyperlink{p:second.moment.judicious.proof}{proof of Proposition~\ref{p:second.moment.judicious}}, we will lower bound $\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)$, and use the above identity to deduce a lower bound on $\mathcal{H}(\nu)-\mathcal{H}(\mu)$.


\item Now suppose instead that $\mu$
solves \eqref{e:constrained.opt.abstract},
while 
$\nu(a)=\Lambda(a)/z$
also belongs to $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$,
but no longer with the assumption that $\ln\Lambda$ are the Lagrange multipliers for $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$.
Then Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy} gives
	\begin{equation}\label{e:rel.entropy.bound.by.weights}
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	=\Bigg\{
	\underbrace{\mathcal{H}(\nu)- \mathcal{H}(\mu)
	}_{\textup{nonpositive}}
	\Bigg\}
	+\Big\langle \nu-\mu,\ln\Lambda\Big\rangle
	\le
	\Big\langle \nu-\mu,\ln\Lambda\Big\rangle\,,
	\end{equation}
where the inequality holds since $\mathcal{H}(\mu)\ge\mathcal{H}(\nu)$ by assumption. 
If $\Lambda$ are the Lagrange multipliers for $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$, then 
$\langle\mu,\ln\Lambda\rangle$ must be constant over $\mu\in\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$, so we would have
$\langle\nu-\mu,\ln\Lambda\rangle=0$ and thus
$\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)=0$, as already noted.
However, in some of the applications that follow, we will only have $\Lambda$ ``close to'' the Lagrange multipliers for $\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$, such that 
$\langle\mu,\ln\Lambda\rangle$ is
roughly constant over $\mu\in\textup{\textsf{LIN}}$.
This will imply that
$\langle\nu-\mu,\ln\Lambda\rangle$
is small, so that $\mu$ must be close to $\nu$. 
For the formal details, see the proof of
Proposition~\ref{p:defect.join}. 
\end{enumerate}
We emphasize that although Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy} is a trivial identity, it plays an essential part in the proof of the key results of this paper.

 By the result of Lemma~\ref{l:if.neg.def}, it suffices to show that when we restrict to $\omega\in\bm{I}_0$, the function $\bm{\Psi}=\bm{\Psi}_{\mathscr{D},2}$ is uniquely maximized at $\omega(\mathscr{D})={}_{*}\omega$ (in the notation of \eqref{e:opt.omega.of.DD}), with negative-definite Hessian at the maximizer. For the proof, we assume that $\mathscr{D}$ is bounded away from zero in the sense of \eqref{e:pos.frac}; this holds with high probability by Proposition~\ref{p:posfrac}. We also assume that the profile $\mathscr{D}$ expands on type-subsets, which by Lemma~\ref{l:expand.on.types} occurs with high probability. Now take any maximizer $\omega=\omega(\mathscr{D})$ from \eqref{e:opt.omega.of.DD} --- we have not yet argued that it is unique. By the expansion condition and the \textit{a~priori} estimate Proposition~\ref{p:apriori} (and recalling from Definition~\ref{d:error.notation.edge} that $\zeta=\zeta_*/4$), we know that $\omega$ satisfies the estimate \eqref{e:apriori} on all $(\bm{L},j)$ such that $\bm{L}(j)$ is a strongly non-defective edge type in the sense of Remark~\ref{r:defective.clauses}. \smallskip

\noindent\bemph{Proof that $\omega(\mathscr{D})={}_{*}\omega$
	uniquely maximizes
	$\bm{\Psi}=\bm{\Psi}_{\mathscr{D},2}$ on $\bm{I}_0$.}
We start by letting $\omega=\omega(\mathscr{D})$ be any maximizer from
\eqref{e:opt.omega.of.DD}. Consequently, in Proposition~\ref{p:update.compound},
for any choice of compound enclosure $U$,
on the left-hand side of
\eqref{e:max.over.acute.omega.compound}
the maximum over $\acute{\omega}$ must be
$\bm{\Psi}(\acute{\omega})
-\bm{\Psi}(\omega)=0$. 
Therefore the right-hand side of
\eqref{e:max.over.acute.omega.compound} also vanishes:
	\[
	\max_{\nu'}
	\Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\nu')
		: 	\nu' \in
		\bm{J}(U,\omega_{\delta U})
		\Bigg\}
	=\max_{\mu'}
	\Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\mu')
		: 	\mu' \in
		\bm{\Delta}(U;\omega_U)\Bigg\}
	\]
Since the entropy function $\mathcal{H}$ is strictly concave on 
$\bm{J}(U,\omega_{\delta U})\supseteq
\bm{\Delta}(U;\omega_U)$, this implies $\mu=\nu$ where 
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\nu
	&=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})
	= \textup{arg\,max}_{\nu'}
	\Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\nu')
		: 	\nu' \in
		\bm{J}(U,\omega_{\delta U})\Bigg\}\,,\\
	\mu
	&= \mu(U;\omega)
	=\textup{arg\,max}_{\mu'}
	\Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\mu')
		: 	\mu' \in
		\bm{\Delta}(U;\omega_U)\Bigg\}\,.
	\label{e:mu.nu}
	\end{align}
(Note that $\mu,\nu$ are uniquely defined by the strict concavity of $\mathcal{H}$.) 
Likewise, by Proposition~\ref{p:block.update.non.compound}, if $U$ is the depth-one neighborhood of any non-compound variable, then we must have
$\mu=\nu$ where
$\nu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$
and $\mu=\mu(U;\omega_U)$
(the only difference being that these are now
 in the augmented pair coloring model).
We now argue that $\omega_{\bm{L},j}={}_{*}\omega_{\bm{L},j}$ in all cases where $\bm{L}$ is nice. Indeed, let
	\begin{equation}\label{e:max.disc.over.nondefective}
	D(\omega) = \max_e
	\Bigg\{
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	: \textup{$e$ is nice}\Bigg\}\,,
	\end{equation}
and let $e_\star$ be any nice edge that attains the maximum. We have two cases:
\begin{enumerate}[1.]
\item Suppose $e_\star$ is an interior edge in a compound enclosure $U$. Recall from
Definition~\ref{d:enclosure} that
the boundary of $U$ consists of variables which are perfect (Definition~\ref{d:perfect.fair}), hence also orderly. We saw in Definition~\ref{d:orderly} that an orderly variable cannot lie within distance $(\delta_*)^3$ of any defect, so every $e\in\delta U$ is strongly non-defective in the sense of Remark~\ref{r:defective.clauses}. Thus Proposition~\ref{p:apriori} can be applied, and guarantees that the condition
\eqref{e:apriori.assump.omega.COMPOUND}
of Proposition~\ref{p:contraction.COMPOUND} is satisfied by every $e\in\delta U$
(again, recall from Definition~\ref{d:error.notation.edge} that we take $\zeta=\zeta_*/4$). Then the result
\eqref{e:block.update.final} of Proposition~\ref{p:contraction.COMPOUND} 
(with $C_{e_\star}=1$, since $e_\star$ is nice)
gives
that $\nu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$ must satisfy
	\[
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\le
	\frac14\Bigg\{
	\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg\}
	\le \frac{D(\omega)}{4}\,,
	\]
where the last bound uses that all edges of $\delta U$ must be nice. On the other hand, as we noted
in \eqref{e:mu.nu} above,
$\nu$ must coincide with $\mu=\mu(U;\omega)$
by Proposition~\ref{p:update.compound}, so in fact we must have
	\[
	D(\omega) =\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)=\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu) 
	\le \frac{D(\omega)}{4}\,.
	\]
This proves $D(\omega)=0$ for the case that $e$ lies in the interior of a compound enclosure.

\item Suppose instead that $e_\star=(av)$ where $v$ is a non-compound variable. It follows from Definition~\ref{d:enclosure} that $v$ must be perfect, so by the same reasoning as above it cannot lie within distance $(\delta_*)^3$ of any defect. 
Thus, if $U$ is the depth-one neighborhood of $v$,
every $e\in\delta U$ must be strongly non-defective
in the sense of Remark~\ref{r:defective.clauses}. It follows by Proposition~\ref{p:apriori} that the condition~\eqref{e:apriori.assump.omega.Augmented}
of Proposition~\ref{e:apriori.assump.omega.Augmented} is satisfied for every $e\in\delta U$
(again using that $\zeta=\zeta_*/4$). Then, the result
\eqref{e:depth.one.update.final} of Proposition~\ref{p:contraction.for.simple.var}
(or its consequence \eqref{e:depth.one.update.final.maxversion}) implies that $\nu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$ must satisfy
	\[
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\le k^{O(1)} (\vartheta_*)^{1/4}
	\Bigg\{\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg\}
	\le k^{O(1)} (\vartheta_*)^{1/4}
	D(\omega) \,,
	\]
where the last bound again uses that all edges of $\delta U$ must be nice. On the other hand, we again have that $\nu$ must coincide with $\mu=\mu(U;\omega)$ by Proposition~\ref{p:block.update.non.compound}, so in fact we obtain the bound
	\[
	D(\omega)
	=\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)
	=\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\le \frac{D(\omega)}{4}\,.
	\]
This proves $D(\omega)=0$ for the case that $e$
does not lie in the interior of a compound enclosure.
\end{enumerate}
The above proves $D(\omega)=0$, i.e., the optimizer $\omega=\omega(\mathscr{D})$ coincides with the canonical product measure ${}_{*}\omega$ on all nice edges. If $e$ is a non-nice edge, then it must lie in the interior of a compound enclosure $U$. In this case, the result \eqref{e:block.update.final} of Proposition~\ref{p:contraction.COMPOUND} gives,
with $C$ as in \eqref{e:choice.of.C},
	\begin{equation}\label{e:defective.nonquant.bound}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\le \frac{C}{4}\Bigg\{
	\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg\}
	\le \frac{C D(\omega)}{4} = 0\,,
	\end{equation}
again using that all edges of $\delta U$ must be nice. This proves that $\omega=\omega(\mathscr{D})$ coincides with ${}_{*}\omega$ on all edges, so that
${}_{*}\omega$ is the unique maximizer of $\bm{\Psi}$ on $\bm{I}_0$ as claimed. 
\smallskip

\noindent\bemph{Proof that $\bm{\Psi}=\bm{\Psi}_{\mathscr{D},2}$ has negative-definite Hessian at ${}_{*}\omega$.}
Now suppose that $\omega\in\bm{I}_0$ is close in euclidean norm to the optimizer $\omega(\mathscr{D})={}_{*}\omega$. 
 As in \eqref{e:max.disc.over.nondefective}, 
let $D(\omega)$ denote the maximal discrepancy under $\omega$ over all nice edges, and let $e_\star$
be any nice edge with $\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)=D(\omega)$. If $e_\star$ lies in the interior of a compound enclosure then let $U$ be that enclosure. If instead $e_\star=(av)$ for a non-compound variable $v$, then let $U$ be the depth-one neighborhood of $v$. In either case we let
$\nu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$
and $\mu=\mu(U;\omega_U)$, as in \eqref{e:mu.nu}. Note that since $\mathcal{H}$ is strictly concave,
 if $\omega$ is very close to ${}_{*}\omega$ then $\nu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$ must be very close to ${}_{*}\nu\equiv\nu^\textup{op}(U;{}_{*}\omega_{\delta U})$, and likewise $\mu=\mu(U;\omega_U)$ must be very close to ${}_{*}\mu\equiv\mu(U;{}_{*}\omega_U)$. But since we proved above that ${}_{*}\omega$ is the unique optimizer for $\bm{\Psi}$ on $\bm{I}_0$, we must have 
${}_{*}\nu={}_{*}\mu$, and therefore $\mu$ must be very close to $\nu$. Next, by Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy} and the first calculation \eqref{e:relative.entropy.with.Lagrangian} in Remark~\ref{r:relative.entropy}, we have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:relent.first.app}
	\mathcal{H}(\nu)-\mathcal{H}(\mu)
	=\Bigg\{\mathcal{H}(\nu)-\mathcal{H}(\mu)\Bigg\}
	+\underbrace{
	\Big\langle\ln\Lambda, \nu-\mu\Big\rangle
	}_{\textup{zero}}
	= \mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	\ge
	\max_e
	\mathcal{H}(\mu_e\,|\,\nu_e)
	\,,\end{equation}
where $\Lambda=\Psi(U;\omega_{\delta U})$ from Proposition~\ref{p:contraction.COMPOUND}
in the compound case,
and $\Lambda=\Psi_{\mathscr{D}}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$
from Proposition~\ref{p:contraction.for.simple.var}
in the non-compound case. In the compound case, for $\mu$ sufficiently close to $\nu$, we have
	\[\mathcal{H}(\mu_{e_\star}\,|\,\nu_{e_\star})
	=\sum_\sigma
	\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma)\ln\frac{\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
		{\nu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
	\ge \frac13
	\sum_{\sigma}
	\frac{(\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma)-\nu_{e_\star}(\sigma))^2}
		{\nu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
	\ge \frac13 \Big(\|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty\Big)^2\,.
	\]
In the non-compound case we can instead bound
	\begin{equation}\label{e:relent.lbd.worst.edge}
	\mathcal{H}(\mu_{e_\star}\,|\,\nu_{e_\star})
	=\sum_{\sigma,\bm{L}}
	\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma,\bm{L})\ln\frac{\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma,\bm{L})}
		{\nu_{e_\star}(\sigma,\bm{L})}
	\ge \frac13
	\Big(\|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty\Big)^2\,.
	\end{equation}
We remark that $\mu_e=\omega_e$ for all edges $e$ in $U$, since $\mu=\mu(U;\omega)$. Next, in the compound case we have from \eqref{e:def.discrepancy.measure.e} 
and the triangle inequality that
	\begin{align*}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)
	&=\sum_\sigma
	(\vartheta_*)^{\Ind{\SSPIN{r}[\sigma]\ge1}}
	\Bigg| \frac{\omega_{e_\star}(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\omega_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
			-1
		\Bigg|
	=\sum_\sigma
	(\vartheta_*)^{\Ind{\SSPIN{r}[\sigma]\ge1}}
	\Bigg| \frac{\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\omega_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
			-1
		\Bigg|\\
	&\le
	\underbrace{\sum_\sigma
	(\vartheta_*)^{\Ind{\SSPIN{r}[\sigma]\ge1}}
	\Bigg| \frac{\nu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\omega_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
			-1
		\Bigg|}_{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)}
	+\underbrace{\sum_\sigma
	(\vartheta_*)^{\Ind{\SSPIN{r}[\sigma]\ge1}}
	\Bigg|
	\frac{\mu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\omega_{e_\star}(\sigma)}-
	 \frac{\nu_{e_\star}(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\omega_{e_\star}(\sigma)}
		\Bigg|}_{\le
			{C_1(k)}
			\|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty}\,,
	\end{align*}
where $C_1(k)$ is a constant depending only on $k$ (this uses the $e_\star$ is a nice edge). Rearranging gives
	\[
	\frac1{C_1(k)}
	\Bigg\{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)
	-\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\Bigg\}
	\le \|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty\,.\]
Similarly, in the non-compound case we have from
\eqref{e:disc.augmented} and the triangle inequality that
	\begin{equation}\label{e:disc.triangle.ineq}
	\frac1{C_1}
	\Bigg\{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)
	-\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\Bigg\}
	\le \|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty
	\end{equation}
where $C_1$ depends only on $k$ and on the constant $c_1$ from Proposition~\ref{p:posfrac}.
By adjusting $C_1$ we can assume it is larger than the $C_1(k)$ from the preceding bound, so that 
\eqref{e:disc.triangle.ineq} holds for $e_\star$ in both the compound and non-compound cases. Next, by the contraction results from Propositions~\ref{p:contraction.for.simple.var}
and \ref{p:contraction.COMPOUND}, we have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:apply.contraction}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\le
	\frac14
	\max_e\Bigg\{
	 \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	 : \textup{$e$ is nice}\Bigg\}
	= \frac14 \textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)\,,
	\end{equation}
where the last equality holds because $e_\star$ was chosen to maximize $\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)$ over all nice edges. (Observe that \eqref{e:apply.contraction} crucially uses the restriction to nice edges;
we recall from \eqref{e:block.update.final} that we have an extra factor $C$ (from \eqref{e:choice.of.C})
for non-nice edges.) It follows that
	\begin{equation}\label{e:main.result.of.contraction}
	\|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:disc.triangle.ineq}}{\ge} 
	\frac1{C_1}
	\Bigg\{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)
	-\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\nu)
	\Bigg\}
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:apply.contraction}}{\ge} 
	\frac3{4 C_1} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\omega)
	=\frac{3D(\omega)}{4 C_1} 
	\,.
	\end{equation}
Next, let $e_{\max}$ be the edge $e$ with maximal discrepancy $\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)=D_{\max}(\omega)$ (this is over all edges, both nice and non-nice). If $e_{\max}$ is nice, then $D_{\max}(\omega)=D(\omega)$. 
More generally, if $D_{\max}(\omega) \le C^3 D(\omega)$ for $C$ as in \eqref{e:choice.of.C}, then the preceding bound \eqref{e:main.result.of.contraction} readily implies
	\begin{equation}\label{e:main.result.of.contraction.nice}
	\|\mu_{e_\star}-\nu_{e_\star}\|_\infty
	\ge \frac{3D(\omega)}{4 C_1} 
	\ge \frac{3D_{\max}(\omega)}{4 C_1 C^3}\,. 
	\end{equation}
Now suppose instead that $D_{\max}(\omega) \ge C^3 D(\omega)$. Then
	\[
	\|\nu_{e_{\max}}-{}_{*}\omega_{e_{\max}}\|_\infty
	\le \frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_{\max}}(\nu)}{(\vartheta_*)^2}
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:block.update.final}}
		{\le}
	\frac{C D(\omega)}{4(\vartheta_*)^2}
	\stackrel{\odot}{\le}
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_{\max}}(\omega)}{4(\vartheta_*)^2 C^2}
	\le \frac{\|\omega_{e_{\max}}-{}_{*}\omega_{e_{\max}}
		\|_\infty}{4(\vartheta_*)^2 C}\,.
	\]
(In the above, the bound marked $\odot$ uses 
 the assumed lower bound on $D_{\max}(\omega)$.
 The last bound uses the definition \eqref{e:choice.of.C} of $C$.)
Since $(\vartheta_*)^2 C$ is a large constant, we can conclude for this case that
	\begin{equation}\label{e:main.result.of.contraction.bad}
	\|\mu_{e_{\max}}-\nu_{e_{\max}}\|_\infty
	\ge
	\|\omega_{e_{\max}}-{}_{*}\omega_{e_{\max}}\|_\infty
	-\|\nu_{e_{\max}}-{}_{*}\omega_{e_{\max}}\|_\infty
	\ge
	\frac{\|\omega_{e_{\max}}-{}_{*}\omega_{e_{\max}}\|_\infty}
		{2}
	\ge \frac{D_{\max}(\omega)}{C}\,.
	\end{equation}
Combining \eqref{e:main.result.of.contraction.nice} and \eqref{e:main.result.of.contraction.bad} gives,
for some constant $C_2=C_2(k,R)$, the lower bound
	\begin{equation}\label{e:main.result.of.contraction.final}
	\max_e\|\mu_e-\nu_e\|_\infty
	\ge \frac{D_{\max}(\omega)}{C_2}\,.
	\end{equation}
Since we assumed that each clause type $\bm{L}$ occurs at least $c_1$ fraction of times, the total number of indices $(\bm{L},j)$ is at most $k/c_1$, so the euclidean distance between $\omega$ and ${}_{*}\omega$ can be upper bounded as 
	\begin{equation}\label{e:euclidean.to.max}
	\Big(\|\omega-{}_{*}\omega\|_2\Big)^2
	= \sum_{\bm{L},j}
		\Big(\|\omega_{\bm{L},j}-{}_{*}\omega_{\bm{L},j}\|_2\Big)^2
	\le
	\frac{C_3 k}{c_1}
	\Bigg( \max_e \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)\Bigg)^2
	\le\frac{C_3 k}{c_1} D_{\max}(\omega)^2
	\,.\end{equation}
Combining the bounds obtained so far gives 
	\begin{align*}
	\mathcal{H}(\nu)-\mathcal{H}(\mu)
	&\stackrel{\eqref{e:relent.first.app}}{\ge}
	\max_e \mathcal{H}(\mu_e\,|\,\nu_e)
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:relent.lbd.worst.edge}}{\ge}
	\max_e\frac{(\|\mu_e-\nu_e\|_\infty)^2}{3}
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:main.result.of.contraction.final}}{\ge}
	\frac13\bigg(
		\frac{D_{\max}(\omega)}{C_3}
		\bigg)^2
	\\
	&\stackrel{\eqref{e:euclidean.to.max}}{\ge}
	\frac13 \frac{c_1(\|\omega-{}_{*}\omega\|_2)^2}
	{C_3 k (C_2)^2 }
	\equiv \frac1{C_4}
	\Big(\|\omega-{}_{*}\omega\|_2\Big)^2\,.
	\end{align*}
To conclude, let $\omega_\nu\in\bm{I}_0$ be the edge empirical marginals of $\nu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$. Since we already showed that ${}_{*}\omega$ optimizes $\bm{\Psi}$ over $\bm{I}_0$, we must have
$\bm{\Psi}({}_{*}\omega) \ge \bm{\Psi}(\omega_\nu)$.
Combining with the results of Propositions~\ref{p:update.compound} and \ref{p:block.update.non.compound} gives
	\[
	\bm{\Psi}({}_{*}\omega)-\bm{\Psi}(\omega)
	\ge \bm{\Psi}(\omega_\nu)-\bm{\Psi}(\omega)
	\ge c_1
	\Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\nu)-\mathcal{H}(\mu)
	\Bigg\}
	\ge \frac{c_1}{C_4}
	\Big(\|\omega-{}_{*}\omega\|_2\Big)^2
	\,.
	\]
This proves that $\bm{\Psi}$ has negative-definite Hessian at ${}_{*}\omega$, as claimed. As noted above, the result follows by applying Lemma~\ref{l:if.neg.def}.

 Fix any edge $e_\star=(av)$ in $U\setminus\delta U$, and decompose $U$ as in Definition~\ref{d:joins} around $v_\star=v$. As in Proposition~\ref{p:nondefect.join}, consider a merge step where the uppermost join $J$ is non-defective. Let $T$ be the merged tree, and $\mu=\nu^\textup{op}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$. If $J$ contains $v_\star$, then $T=U$ and $\mu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$. If $J$ does not contain $v_\star$, then $T$ is rooted at an edge $e_\textup{\textsc{rt}}=(a_\textup{\textsc{rt}}v_\textup{\textsc{rt}})$, and indeed 
	\[
	\mu
	=\nu^\textup{op}(T;\omega_{\delta T})
	=\nu^\textup{op}(T_{e_\textup{\textsc{rt}}};\omega_{\delta T_{e_\textup{\textsc{rt}}}})
	=\nu(e_\textup{\textsc{rt}})\,,
	\]
where $\nu(e_\textup{\textsc{rt}})$ is defined analogously to the measure $\nu(e)$ from Definition~\ref{d:merge.joins}. 
In either case, the marginal of $\mu$ on $J$ must be given by $\mu=\nu^\textup{op}(J;\mu_{\delta J})$. It follows by 
Proposition~\ref{p:nice.tree.lagrange} that
for any edge $(au)$ in $J$ we have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:contraction.in.good.J}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{au}(\mu)
	\le
	k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e\in\delta J}
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathfrak{b}_J(e,a)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\,.
	\end{equation}
(To be pedantic, \eqref{e:contraction.in.good.J} follows directly from Proposition~\ref{p:nice.tree.lagrange} in the case that $J$ is rooted at an edge $e_\textup{\textsc{rt}}$. In the final merge step where $J$ is rooted at the variable $v_\star$, \eqref{e:contraction.in.good.J} follows from the variable-rooted analogue of Proposition~\ref{p:nice.tree.lagrange} --- this can be obtained by an extremely similar proof, so we will not elaborate on it here.) Recall that $\delta J$ denotes the set of edges in $J$ incident to all the leaf variables of $J$, while $\delta'J$ is the subset that are incident to terminal variables. If $e\in\delta J\setminus\delta'J$, then $\mu_e=\omega_e$. On the other hand, the contribution to the above sum from $e\in\delta'J$ is bounded by
Proposition~\ref{p:nondefect.join}. It follows that
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{au}(\mu)
	&\le k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e\in\delta J\setminus\delta'J}
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathfrak{b}_J(e,a)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	+k^{O(1)} 4^{k\mathcal{M}}
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}^2}
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\\
	&\le
	k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e\in\delta J\setminus\delta'J}
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/5}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathfrak{b}_J(e,a)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	+k^{O(1)} 
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/5}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}^2}
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\,,
	\label{e:recursive.disc.bound.good.case}
	\end{align}
where the last bound holds for $\mathcal{M}$ large enough: to be precise, we require
	\[
	4^{k\mathcal{M}}
	\le\bigg( \frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/5}}{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}^2}
	=\bigg( \frac1{(\vartheta_*)^{1/20}}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}^2}
	= 2^{k\zeta \mathcal{M}^2/120}\,,
	\]
where the last equality uses that $\vartheta_*\equiv 2^{-k\zeta/6}$ from Definition~\ref{d:error.notation.edge}. Thus it suffices here to have 
	\[\kappa_*
	\equiv \mathcal{M}^4
	\ge \bigg( \frac{240}{\zeta}\bigg)^4\,.
	\]
Next note for $e\in\delta J$, the discrepancy $\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))$ can be bounded either by
a recursive application of \eqref{e:recursive.disc.bound.good.case} if $e$ is the root of another non-defective join, or by Proposition~\ref{p:defect.join} if $e$ is the root of a defective join. Altogether it gives that for
the edge $e_\star=(av)$ where $v=v_\star$ (and with $C_{e_\star}$ as in \eqref{e:choice.of.C}), 
the maximal-entropy measure
$\mu=\nu^\textup{op}(U;\omega_{\delta U})$
for the full enclosure satisfies 
	\[
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\mu)}{C_{e_\star}}
	\le
	\sum_{e\in\delta U}
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/5}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathfrak{b}_U(e,a)}
		\Bigg\{
		2^{5k} \bigg(
		\frac{2^k}{(\vartheta_*)^{1/5}}
		\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}^2}
		\Bigg\}^{\mathfrak{B}(v_\star,e)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)\,,
	\]
where $\mathfrak{B}(v_\star,e)$ is the number of defective variables between $v_\star$ and $e$, and is a crude upper bound on the number of defective joins intersecting the path between $v_\star$ and $e$. The above can be upper bounded as
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\mu)}{C_{e_\star}}
	&\le
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg)
	\sum_{u\in\partial_\circ U}
		\frac{
		(2^{2k\mathcal{M}^2})^{\mathfrak{B}(v_\star,u)}
		}
		{(2^k (\vartheta_*)^{-1/5})^{d(v_\star,u)}}\\
	&\le 
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg)
	\sum_{u\in\partial_\circ U}
	\frac{ \exp\{ k(\delta_*)^{-1}
		\mathfrak{B}(v_\star,v) \} }
	{ \exp\{ (k\ln2)
		(1+\delta_*) d(v_\star,v) \}}\,,
	\label{e:constrain.kappa.delta}
	\end{align}
where for the last inequality
in \eqref{e:constrain.kappa.delta} to hold we make the following choices. First, in the numerator 
we can ensure that 
$2^{2\mathcal{M}^2}$ is upper bounded by
$\exp(1/\delta_*)$ by requiring
	\[
	\frac{\ln2}{2} \ge \mathcal{M}^2 \delta_* 
	= (\kappa_*)^{1/2} \delta_* \,.\]
In the denominator we can ensure that 
$(\vartheta_*)^{-1/5} = 2^{k\zeta/30}$ upper bounds $2^{k\delta_*}$ by requiring
	\[
	\delta_* \le \frac{\zeta}{30}\,.
	\]
These choices together guarantee the last bound in \eqref{e:constrain.kappa.delta}. It follows by combining with the 
definition of a compound enclosure (cf.~\eqref{eq-def-rad}) that
	\[\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\mu)}{C_{e_\star}}
	\le \frac14
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in\delta U}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg)\,.\]
This proves
the result.

\begin{align}\nonumber
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e_\star}(\mu)}{C_{e_\star}}
	&\le
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg)
	\sum_{u\in\partial_\circ U}
		\frac{
		(2^{2k\mathcal{M}^2})^{\mathfrak{B}(v_\star,u)}
		}
		{(2^k (\vartheta_*)^{-1/5})^{d(v_\star,u)}}\\
	&\le 
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in\delta U} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\omega)
	\Bigg)
	\sum_{u\in\partial_\circ U}
	\frac{ \exp\{ k(\delta_*)^{-1}
		\mathfrak{B}(v_\star,v) \} }
	{ \exp\{ (k\ln2)
		(1+\delta_*) d(v_\star,v) \}}\,,
	\label{e:constrain.kappa.delta}
	\end{align}

Recall from Definition~\ref{d:merge.joins}
the root of $J$ is either $v_\textup{\textsc{rt}}=v_\star$, 
or it is an edge $(a_\textup{\textsc{rt}}v_\textup{\textsc{rt}})$.
In the first case we let ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Lambda_J$ be as given by
Corollary~\ref{c:clause.bp.weights}. In the second case we let ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Lambda_J$ be given by
\eqref{e:Lmstar.T.short.defn} from Remark~\ref{r:adjusted.bp.stability},
i.e., we give weight ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\hat{q}_{a_\textup{\textsc{rt}}v_\textup{\textsc{rt}}}$ to the root clause $a_\textup{\textsc{rt}}$. Note that ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Lambda_J$ includes a weight ${}_\bullet\hspace{-1pt}\dot{q}_e$ for each $e\in\delta J$. Therefore, for a single-copy coloring $\smash{\vec{\tau}}_{J\setminus\delta J}$ on $J\setminus\delta J$, we can let
	\[
	{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\tau}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\equiv {}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Lambda_J(\smash{\vec{\tau}}_J)
		\prod_{e\in\delta J}
	\frac1{{}_\bullet\hspace{-1pt}\dot{q}_e(\tau_e)}\,,
	\]
where this is well-defined because the right-hand side does not depend on $\smash{\vec{\tau}}_{\delta J}$. We then let 
${}_{*}\Theta\equiv{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Theta\otimes{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\Theta$.

Next, recall from Remark~\ref{r:boundaries.of.joins} that $\delta J=\delta'J$ is the set of boundary edges of $J$. Let $A$ be the subset of clauses in $J$ that are incident to $\delta J$, so
$A$ is a subset of the set $\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{X})$
from Definition~\ref{d:joins}. Let $e=(au)\in\delta J$, so $a\in A$ and $u$ is a terminal variable. Recall that $T_e$ is the subtree of $U$ descended from $e$. Let $\Lambda_{T_e}=\Lambda(T_e;\omega_{\delta T_e})$ be the Lagrangian weights on $T_e$ such that
$\nu(e)=\nu^\textup{op}(T_e,\omega_{\delta T_e})$
agrees with $\nu[T_e;\Lambda_{T_e}]$. We assume moreover that they are parametrized as in Definition~\ref{d:param.weights.Lambda}, meaning that the root clause $a(e)$ of $T_e$ receives weight ${}_{*}\hat{q}_e$ under $\Lambda_{T_e}$. Write $q\equiv q_\textup{\textsc{bp}}(T_e;\Lambda_{T_e})$ for the associated \textsc{bp} messages, so in particular the downward message on $e$ is
 $\hat{q}_e={}_{*}\hat{q}_e$. It will follow by induction that
 the upward message
 $\dot{q}_e$ satisfies the bounds \eqref{e:first.update.at.boundary}
for each edge $e\in \delta J$.\smallskip

\noindent
\textbf{Step 1. Weights on merged tree.}
We now apply Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights}
to each clause $a\in A$ where we take incoming messages
$\dot{q}_{ua}$ from below as just discussed,
but the canonical product message $\dot{q}_{va}={}_{*}\dot{q}_{va}$ from the parent variable $v$.
Let $h$ denote the resulting outgoing messages from the unweighted clause $a$, that is,
 $h=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_a[\dot{q}]$.
Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights}
gives a clause weighting
$\Gamma_a\equiv (\gamma_e)_{e\in\delta a}$ such that for these input messages to $a$, the $\Gamma_a$-weighted clause \textsc{bp} recursion \bemph{outputs the canonical product message} ${}_{*}\hat{q}$ on every edge in $\delta a$. For a pair configuration $\smash{\vec{\sigma}}\equiv\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_T$ on the merged tree $T$, we define the weight
	\[
	\Theta(\smash{\vec{\sigma}})
	\equiv
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\prod_{a\in A} \Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\Bigg\}
	\Bigg\{
	\prod_{e\in \delta J}
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\Bigg\}\,.
	\]
Recall from above that the clause $a(e)$ receives weight ${}_{*}\hat{q}_e$ under $\Lambda_{T_e}$, and in the above expression we divided by $\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e)$ to remove this weight. Likewise, ${}_{*}\Lambda_J$ includes a weight ${}_{*}\dot{q}_e$ for each $e\in\delta J$, and we also divided by ${}_{*}\dot{q}_e(\sigma_e)$ to remove this weight. Let $\nu\equiv\nu[T;\Theta]$.
Let $e(a)$ denote the parent edge of $a$, i.e., the unique element of $\delta a\setminus\delta J$.
 The marginal of $\nu$ on $J\setminus\delta J$ is
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\nu_{J\setminus\delta J}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	&\cong
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta J}}
	\prod_{a\in A}\Bigg(
	\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\prod_{e\in\delta a\setminus e(a)}
	\bigg[
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e\setminus J}}
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e)}
		\bigg]
	\Bigg)\Bigg\}\\ \nonumber
	&\stackrel{\textit{(a)}}{\cong}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\prod_{a\in A}\Bigg(
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a \setminus e(a)}}
	\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\prod_{e\in\delta a\setminus e(a)} \dot{q}_e(\sigma_e)
	\Bigg)\Bigg\}\\
	&\stackrel{\textit{(b)}}{\cong}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{ \prod_{e'\in\delta A\setminus\delta J}
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'})
	\Bigg\}
	\cong
	{}_{*}\nu_{J\setminus\delta J}
	(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})\,,
	\label{e:Theta.marginal.A}
	\end{align}
where ${}_{*}\nu={}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\nu\otimes{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\nu$ is the canonical product measure. (In the above calculation, the step marked \textit{(a)} uses that under the $\Lambda_{T_e}$-weighted measure on $T_e$, the marginal on edge $e$ is proportional to ${}_{*}\hat{q}_e\dot{q}_e$. The step marked \textit{(b)} uses that the $\Gamma_a$-weighted \textsc{bp} recursion outputs the canonical product message ${}_{*}\hat{q}$ on the edge $e(a)$.) Similarly, for any $e=(bu)\in\delta J$,
 the marginal of $\nu$ on $T_e$ is given by
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\nu_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})
	&\cong
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J}}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b	\setminus\set{e,e(b)}}}
	\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\prod_{e'\in\delta b\setminus\set{e,e(b)}}
	\bigg[
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e'} \setminus J}}
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_{e'}}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e'}})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'}) }
	\bigg]
	\Bigg\}\\ \nonumber
	&\qquad\times\Bigg\{
	\prod_{a\in A\setminus b}
	\Bigg(
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a\setminus e(a)}}
		\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
		\prod_{e''\in\delta a\setminus e(a)}
		\bigg[
		\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e''} \setminus J}}
		\frac{\Lambda_{T_{e''}}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e''}})}
			{{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e''}(\sigma_{e''}) }
		\bigg]
	\Bigg)\Bigg\}\\ \nonumber
	&\cong
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J}}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b	\setminus \set{e,e(b)}}}
	\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\prod_{e'\in\delta b
		\setminus \set{e,e(b)}}
	\dot{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'})
	\Bigg\}
	\prod_{e''\in\delta A\setminus(\delta J\cup\delta b)}
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e''}(\sigma_{e''})\\
	&\cong
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b	\setminus e}}
	\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\Bigg\{
	{}_{*}\dot{q}_{e(b)}(\sigma_{e'})
	\prod_{e'\in\delta b
		\setminus \set{e,e(b)}}
	\dot{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'})
	\Bigg\}
	\cong \Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})\,,
	\label{e:Theta.marginal.B}
	\end{align}
where the last step uses that the $\Gamma_a$-weighted \textsc{bp} recursion outputs the canonical
product message ${}_{*}\hat{q}$ on the edge $e$. This shows that the marginal of $\nu$ on $T_e$ is simply $\nu(e)=\nu^\textup{op}(T_e;\omega_{\delta T_e})$.
It follows that
$\nu\in\bm{J}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$.
Consequently, $\mu=\nu^\textup{op}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$ is the entropy maximizer over
$\bm{J}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$, then
$\mathcal{H}(\mu)\ge\mathcal{H}(\nu)$.
It follows by recalling Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy}
and Remark~\ref{r:relative.entropy} 
(in particular, the calculation
\eqref{e:rel.entropy.bound.by.weights}) that
	\begin{equation}\label{e:entropy.calculation}
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	= \Bigg\{ \mathcal{H}(\nu)-\mathcal{H}(\mu)\Bigg\}
	+\Big\langle \nu-\mu,
		\ln\Theta\Big\rangle
	\le
	\Big\langle \nu-\mu,
		\ln\Theta\Big\rangle.
	\end{equation}
Recall that the weights in $\Theta$ are of product form, except on the boundary edges $\delta T$
and on the edges $\delta A$. Since $\mu$ and $\nu$ agree on the subtrees $T_e$, and have the same single-copy marginals (given by ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi$) on all edges of the merged tree $T$, the only contribution to the right-hand side of 
\eqref{e:entropy.calculation} comes from the 
edges
 $e'\in\delta A\setminus\delta J$:
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	&\le\adjustlimits
	\sum_{e'\in\delta A \setminus\delta J}
	\sum_\sigma
	\Bigg|
		\bigg\{
		\nu_{e'}(\sigma)
		-\mu_{e'}(\sigma)\bigg\}
		\ln \gamma_{e'}(\sigma)
		\Bigg|\\
	&\le\adjustlimits
	\sum_{e'\in\delta A \setminus\delta J}
	\|\ln\gamma_{e'}\|_\infty
	\|\mu_{e'}-{}_{*}\omega_{e'}\|_\infty\,,
	\label{e:entropy.ubd.by.contribution.from.bdy}
	\end{align}
where the last step uses that 
$\nu_{e'}={}_{*}\omega_{e'}$ for all $e'\in J\setminus \delta J$. \smallskip

\noindent\bemph{Step 2. Bound on clause weights.}
We first bound the $\ln\gamma_{e'}$ term in \eqref{e:entropy.ubd.by.contribution.from.bdy}.
For each $a\in A$, there is a single edge $e'=e(a)$ in $\delta a\setminus \delta J$. A crude application of Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights} gives
	\begin{equation}\label{e:log.gamma.crude.bound.eps}
	\|\ln\gamma_{e(a)}\|_\infty
	\le
	k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e''\in\delta a \setminus e(a)}
	\bigg(
	\epsilon_{e''}+\dot{\epsilon}_{e''}+\ddot{\epsilon}_{e''}
	\bigg)\,,
	\end{equation}
where $\vec{\epsilon}=(\epsilon_{e''},\dot{\epsilon}_{e''},\ddot{\epsilon}_{e''})
=\widehat{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize ERR}}}({}_{*}\hat{q}_{e''},h_{e''})$
 is the error between 
the unweighted messages $h=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_a[\dot{q}]$
and the weighted messages ${}_{*}\hat{q}=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_a[\dot{q};\Gamma_a]=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_a[{}_{*}\dot{q}]$. A crude application of Proposition~\ref{p:clause.update} 
gives, for $e''\in\delta a\setminus e(a)$,
	\begin{equation}\label{e:log.gamma.crude.bound.delta}
	\epsilon_{e''}+\dot{\epsilon}_{e''}+\ddot{\epsilon}_{e''}
	\le
	k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e\in\delta a\setminus e(a)}
	\bigg(
	\delta_e
	+\dot{\delta}_e
	+\ddot{\delta}_e
	+{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\delta_e
	+{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\dot{\delta}_e
	\bigg)\,,
	\end{equation}
where $\vec{\delta}=(\delta_e,\dot{\delta}_e,\ddot{\delta}_e,{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\delta_e,{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\dot{\delta}_e)=\dddot{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize ERR}}}({}_{*}\dot{q}_e,\dot{q}_e)$.
To bound the error between $\dot{q}_e$ and ${}_{*}\dot{q}_e$ for $e\in\delta J$, note
	\[
	\dot{q}_e(\sigma)
	\cong \frac{\nu_e(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma)}
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:def.discrepancy.measure.e}}{=}
	\frac{{}_{*}\omega_e(\sigma)}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma)}
	\Bigg\{
	1 + O\bigg(\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu)}{\vartheta_*}\bigg)
	\Bigg\}
	\cong
	{}_{*}\dot{q}_e(\sigma)
	\Bigg\{
	1 + O\bigg(\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu)}{\vartheta_*}\bigg)
	\Bigg\}\,,
	\]
so each entry of $\vec{\delta}$ is
$O((\vartheta_*)^{-1} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu))$.
Combining the last few bounds gives (again very crudely)
	\begin{equation}\label{e:apply.cor.clause.weights}
	\|\ln\gamma_{e(a)}\|_\infty
	\le
	2^k 
	\sum_{e\in\delta a \setminus e(a)}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu)
	=2^k 
	\sum_{e\in\delta a \setminus e(a)}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\,,
	\end{equation}
for each edge $e'\in\delta A\setminus\delta J$.
\smallskip

\noindent\bemph{Step 3. Conclusion.} 
To conclude we note that for each $e\in\delta A$, we have
	\[
	\|\mu_e-{}_{*}\omega_e\|_\infty
	\le
	\|{}_{*}\omega_e\|_\infty
	\Bigg\|\frac{\mu_e}{{}_{*}\omega_e}-1\Bigg\|_\infty
	\le 
	\Bigg\|\frac{\mu_e}{{}_{*}\omega_e}-1\Bigg\|_\infty
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:def.discrepancy.measure.e}}{\le}
	2^k\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\,.
	\]
Combining with \eqref{e:apply.cor.clause.weights}
and substituting into \eqref{e:entropy.ubd.by.contribution.from.bdy} gives
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	&\le 2^{2k} 
		\sum_{a\in A}
		\Bigg(\sum_{e\in\delta a
			\setminus e(a)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
		\Bigg)
		\Bigg(
		\sum_{e'\in \delta a \setminus \delta J}
			\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)
		\Bigg)\\
	&\le 2^{2k}
	\Bigg(\max_{e'\in J\setminus \delta J}
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)}{C_{e'}}
	\Bigg)\Bigg(
	\sum_{e\in\delta J} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\Bigg)
	\label{e:entropy.bd.defective}
	\end{align}
where $C_{e'}$ is as in the statement of the proposition (and the last bound holds simply because $C_{e'}=1$ for all $e'\in\delta A$, since those edges must be nice). On the other hand, for any $e'\in J\setminus\delta J$, we have
	\[
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	\ge \mathcal{H}(\mu_{e'}\,|\,\nu_{e'})
	= \mathcal{H}(\mu_{e'}\,|\,{}_{*}\omega_{e'})
	\ge
	\frac1{2^{3k}}
	\bigg(\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)}{C_{e'}}\bigg)^2\,,
	\]
where we take $C_{e'}$ as in \eqref{e:choice.of.C} to account for non-nice edges where 
the minimum of ${}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi_{e'}$ on its support may be small. Combining the last two displays gives
	\[
	\max_{e'\in J\setminus \delta J}
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)}{C_{e'}}
	\le 2^{5k}
	\sum_{e\in\delta J} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\,,
	\]
as claimed.

\begin{align}\nonumber
	\nu_{J\setminus\delta J}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	&\cong
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta J}}
	\prod_{a\in A}\Bigg(
	\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\prod_{e\in\delta a\setminus e(a)}
	\bigg[
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e\setminus J}}
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e)}
		\bigg]
	\Bigg)\Bigg\}\\ \nonumber
	&\stackrel{\textit{(a)}}{\cong}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\prod_{a\in A}\Bigg(
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a \setminus e(a)}}
	\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\prod_{e\in\delta a\setminus e(a)} \dot{q}_e(\sigma_e)
	\Bigg)\Bigg\}\\
	&\stackrel{\textit{(b)}}{\cong}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{ \prod_{e'\in\delta A\setminus\delta J}
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'})
	\Bigg\}
	\cong
	{}_{*}\nu_{J\setminus\delta J}
	(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})\,,
	\label{e:Theta.marginal.A}
	\end{align}

\begin{align}\nonumber
	\nu_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})
	&\cong
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J}}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b	\setminus\set{e,e(b)}}}
	\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\prod_{e'\in\delta b\setminus\set{e,e(b)}}
	\bigg[
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e'} \setminus J}}
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_{e'}}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e'}})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'}) }
	\bigg]
	\Bigg\}\\ \nonumber
	&\qquad\times\Bigg\{
	\prod_{a\in A\setminus b}
	\Bigg(
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a\setminus e(a)}}
		\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
		\prod_{e''\in\delta a\setminus e(a)}
		\bigg[
		\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e''} \setminus J}}
		\frac{\Lambda_{T_{e''}}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_{e''}})}
			{{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e''}(\sigma_{e''}) }
		\bigg]
	\Bigg)\Bigg\}\\ \nonumber
	&\cong
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J}}
	{}_{*}\Theta_{J\setminus\delta J}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J\setminus\delta J})
	\Bigg\{
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b	\setminus \set{e,e(b)}}}
	\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\prod_{e'\in\delta b
		\setminus \set{e,e(b)}}
	\dot{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'})
	\Bigg\}
	\prod_{e''\in\delta A\setminus(\delta J\cup\delta b)}
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{e''}(\sigma_{e''})\\
	&\cong
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e) }
	\sum_{\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b	\setminus e}}
	\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\Bigg\{
	{}_{*}\dot{q}_{e(b)}(\sigma_{e'})
	\prod_{e'\in\delta b
		\setminus \set{e,e(b)}}
	\dot{q}_{e'}(\sigma_{e'})
	\Bigg\}
	\cong \Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})\,,
	\label{e:Theta.marginal.B}
	\end{align}

\begin{align}\nonumber
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\nu)
	&\le 2^{2k} 
		\sum_{a\in A}
		\Bigg(\sum_{e\in\delta a
			\setminus e(a)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
		\Bigg)
		\Bigg(
		\sum_{e'\in \delta a \setminus \delta J}
			\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)
		\Bigg)\\
	&\le 2^{2k}
	\Bigg(\max_{e'\in J\setminus \delta J}
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)}{C_{e'}}
	\Bigg)\Bigg(
	\sum_{e\in\delta J} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\Bigg)
	\label{e:entropy.bd.defective}
	\end{align}

 Recall that $\mu\equiv\nu^\textup{op}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$, while $\nu(e)=\nu^\textup{op}(T_e;\omega_{\delta T_e})$ for $e\in\delta'J$.\smallskip

\noindent\bemph{Step 1. Identification of small subtree with large discrepancy.} 
For the sake of contradiction, let us suppose the desired bound false, i.e., that we have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:nondefective.join.false.bound}
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	> 4^{k\mathcal{M}}
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\,.
	\end{equation}
Recall from Definition~\ref{d:merge.joins} that 
each variable in $\mathcal{X}\cap\mathcal{L} J$ lies at distance exactly $\mathcal{M}^2$ from the root of $J$, and
$\delta'J$ denotes the edges in $J$ that are incident to $\mathcal{X}\cap\mathcal{L} J$. Similarly to \eqref{e:clause.xi}, for any clause $a$ in $J$ we define
	\[
	\xi_a(J;\mu_{\delta'J})
	\equiv
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}
	\bigg(\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}
		\bigg)^{\mathfrak{b}_J(e,a)}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\,.
	\]
Now let $A$ be the subset of clauses in $J$ at distance exactly $\mathcal{M}$ from $\delta'J$. For any $e\in\delta'J$ and any $j\ge0$, we have
	\[
	\Bigg|\bigg\{a\in A:
	\mathfrak{b}_J(e,a)=\mathcal{M}+j\bigg\}\Bigg|
	\le (dk)^{\lceil j/2 \rceil }\,.
	\]
It follows from this that
	\begin{align}\nonumber
	\sum_{a\in A} \xi_a(J;\mu_{\delta'J})
	&=\sum_{e\in\delta'J}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	\sum_{a\in A}
	\bigg(\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathfrak{b}_T(e,a)}
	\le
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	\sum_{j=0}^\infty
	\bigg(\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}+j}
	(dk)^{\lceil j/2\rceil}\\ \nonumber
	&\le
	\Bigg\{
	\bigg(\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\Bigg\}
	\Bigg(
	1 +2 \sum_{\ell=0}^\infty
		(dk)^\ell
		\bigg(
		\frac{(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}\bigg)^{2\ell-1}
	\Bigg)\\
	&\le \bigg(\frac{k(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
	\sum_{e\in\delta'J}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\,.
	\label{e:nondefective.join.xi.bound}
	\end{align}
For $a\in A$, write $J_a$ for the subtree of $J$ descended from $a$. Write $\delta J_a\equiv J_a\cap\delta'J$. Combining \eqref{e:nondefective.join.false.bound} and \eqref{e:nondefective.join.xi.bound} gives
	\[
	\sum_{a\in A}
	\Bigg\{
	2\sum_{e\in\delta J_a}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	- \bigg( \frac{2^k}{k(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
		\xi_a(J;\mu_{\delta'J})
	- 4^{k\mathcal{M}} \sum_{e\in\delta J_a}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\Bigg\}\ge0\,,
	\]
so we can find some $a\in A$ for which
the expression in braces is non-negative. 
This implies two bounds: first,
	\begin{equation}\label{e:contradiction.a1}
	\xi_a(J;\mu_{\delta'J})
	\le 2 \cdot \bigg( \frac{k(\vartheta_*)^{1/4}}{2^k}
	\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
		\sum_{e\in\delta J_a} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	\le (k^4 (\vartheta_*)^{1/4})^\mathcal{M}
		\Bigg\{
		\max_{e\in\delta'J_a} 	
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\Bigg\}\,,\end{equation}
where the last bound uses that
$|\delta J_a| \le (k^3 2^k)^{\mathcal{M}}$. Secondly, we must also have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:contradiction.a2}
	\max_{e\in\delta J_a}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\le\sum_{e\in\delta J_a}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\le \frac{2}{4^{k\mathcal{M}}}
		\sum_{e\in\delta J_a}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	\le \bigg( \frac{k^4}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
	\Bigg\{\max_{e\in\delta J_a}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\Bigg\}\,.
	\end{equation}
In the remainder of the proof we derive a contradiction.\smallskip

\noindent
\bemph{Step 2. Weights on merged tree.}
First, let $\Lambda=\Lambda_T=\Lambda(T;\omega_{\delta T})$
denote the Lagrangian weights such that $\nu[T;\Lambda]$ coincides with $\mu=\nu^\textup{op}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$.
We write $q$ for the corresponding \textsc{bp} messages. For the distinguished clause $a$, apply
Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights}
to obtain a clause weighting
$\Gamma_a\equiv (\gamma_e)_{e\in\delta a}$
such that
	\begin{align*}
	\hat{q}_{av}
	&=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{av}\bigg[
	\Big({}_{*}\dot{q}_{ua} : u\in\partial a\setminus v\Big)
	;\Gamma_a
	\bigg]\quad
	\textup{for the parent $v$ of $a$,}\\
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{au}
	&=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{au}\bigg[
	\Big(\dot{q}_{av}, 
	({}_{*}\dot{q}_{u'a} : u'
		\in\partial a\setminus\set{u,v} )
	\Big)
	;\Gamma_a
	\bigg]
	\quad
	\textup{for each child $u$ of $a$.}
	\end{align*}
Next, for each $e\in\delta J$,
recall that $\Lambda_{T_e}\equiv \Lambda(T_e;\omega_{\delta T_e})$ denotes the Lagrangian weights such that $\nu[T_e;\Lambda_{T_e}]$ coincides with $\nu(e)=\nu^\textup{op}(T_e;\omega_{\delta T_e})$. We write $\tilde{q}$ for the corresponding \textsc{bp} messages. Let $A_a$ denote the clauses in $J_a$ incident to $\delta J_a$. For each clause $b\in A_a$,
apply Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights} again
to obtain a clause weighting $\Gamma_b=(\gamma_e)_{e\in\delta b}$
such that 
	\begin{align*}
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{bw}
	&=\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{bw}\bigg[
		\Big( \tilde{q}_{ua} : u\in\partial b\setminus w
		\Big)\bigg]
	\quad
	\textup{for the parent variable $w$ of $b$,}\\
	{}_{*}\hat{q}_{bu}
	&= \textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{bu}\bigg[
	\Big( {}_{*}\dot{q}_{wb},
		( \tilde{q}_{u'b} : u'\in\partial b\setminus
			\set{u,w})
		 \Big)
	\bigg]
	\quad
	\textup{for each child $u$ of $b$.}
	\end{align*}
Let $T_a$ denote the subtree of $T$ descended from $a$. Recall that $\Lambda_T=\Lambda(T;\omega_{\delta T})$
is a product of variable factors. Let
$\Lambda_{T,T_a}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_a})$ denote the product
of those factors over the variables in $T_a$ only, and define
	\[
	\Lambda_{T,T\setminus T_a}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T\setminus T_a})
	\equiv 
	\frac{\Lambda_T(\smash{\vec{\sigma}})}{\Lambda_{T,T_a}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_a})}\,.
	\]
For a pair configuration $\smash{\vec{\sigma}}\equiv\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_T$ on the merged tree $T$, we define the weight
	\[
	\Theta(\smash{\vec{\sigma}})
	\equiv
	\Bigg\{
	\Lambda_{T,T\setminus T_a}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T\setminus T_a})
	\Gamma_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\frac{{}_{*}\Theta_{J_a\setminus\delta J_a}
		(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{J_a\setminus\delta J_a})}
		{{}_{*}\hat{q}_{av}(\sigma_{av}) }
	\Bigg\}
	\Bigg\{
	\prod_{b\in A_a}\Gamma_b(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta b})
	\Bigg\}
	\Bigg\{\prod_{e\in\delta J_a}
	\frac{\Lambda_{T_e}(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{T_e})}
		{{}_{*}\hat{q}_e(\sigma_e)}\Bigg\}\,.
	\]
Let $\tilde{\mu}=\nu[T;\Theta]$. It follows by similar calculations as
\eqref{e:Theta.marginal.A}
and \eqref{e:Theta.marginal.B}
that $\tilde{\mu}$ satisfies the following:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item Its marginal on $T\setminus T_a$
	agrees with that of $\mu
			= \nu^\textup{op}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$;
\item Its marginal on $J_a$
	agrees with that of ${}_{*}\nu
		=\nu^\textup{op}(T;{}_{*}\omega_{\delta T})$;
\item 
	Its marginal on $T_e$
	(for each $e\in\delta J_a$)
	agrees with that of
	$\nu(e)
		= \nu^\textup{op}(T_e;\omega_{\delta T_e})$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, $\tilde{\mu}\in\bm{J}(T;\omega_{\delta T})$.
Recalling Lemma~\ref{l:relative.entropy}
and Remark~\ref{r:relative.entropy}
(in particular the calculation \eqref{e:rel.entropy.bound.by.weights}), we have
	\[\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\tilde{\mu})
	=\Bigg\{
	\underbrace{
	\mathcal{H}(\tilde{\mu})- \mathcal{H}(\mu)
	}_{\textup{nonpositive}}
	\Bigg\}
	+\Big\langle \tilde{\mu}-\mu,\ln\Theta\Big\rangle
	\le
	\Big\langle \tilde{\mu}-\mu,
	\ln\Theta\Big\rangle\,.\]
Since $\mu$ and $\tilde{\mu}$ are both judicious, and agree on $T\setminus T_a$, we have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:nondefective.merge.A.B} 
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\tilde{\mu})
	\le \Big\langle \tilde{\mu}-\mu,
	\ln\Theta\Big\rangle 
	\le\Bigg\{
	\underbrace{\sum_{e\in\delta a \cap J_a}
	\Big\langle
	\tilde{\mu}_e-\mu_e, \ln\gamma_e\Big\rangle
	}_{\textup{denote this $\mathcal{A}$}}\Bigg\}
	+\Bigg\{\underbrace{
	\sum_{b\in A_a}
	\sum_{e\in \delta b}
	\Big\langle
	\tilde{\mu}_e-\mu_e, \ln\gamma_e\Big\rangle
	}_{\textup{denote this $\mathcal{B}$}}
	\Bigg\}\,.
	\end{equation}
We now turn to bounding these quantities in terms of
$\textup{\textsf{disc}}(\mu)$ and $\textup{\textsf{disc}}(\nu)$.\smallskip

\noindent
\bemph{Step 3. Bounds on entropy and clause weights.}
Let $e$ be the edge in $J_a$ with maximal $\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)$. Note
	\[\bigg( \mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\tilde{\mu}) \bigg)^{1/2}
	\ge
	\bigg( \mathcal{H}(\mu_e\,|\,\tilde{\mu}_e)
		\bigg)^{1/2}
	\ge
	\frac{\|\mu_e-\tilde{\mu}_e\|_\infty}
		{O(2^k)}
	\ge
	\frac{
	\|\mu_e-{}_{*}\omega_e\|_\infty
	-\|\tilde{\mu}_e-{}_{*}\omega_e\|_\infty
	}{O(2^k)}\,.
	\]
If $e$ belongs to $J_a\setminus\delta J_a$, then
$\tilde{\mu}_e={}_{*}\omega_e$. If $e\in\delta J_a$, then
$\tilde{\mu}_e=\nu(e)_e$, and it follows using \eqref{e:def.discrepancy.measure.e}
 and \eqref{e:contradiction.a2} that
	\[
	\|\tilde{\mu}_e-{}_{*}\omega_e\|_\infty
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:def.discrepancy.measure.e}}
		{\le}
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\tilde{\mu})}{\vartheta_*}
	=\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))}{\vartheta_*}
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:contradiction.a2}}{\le}
	\bigg( \frac{k^4}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
	\frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)}{\vartheta_*}\,.\]
This is negligible in comparison to
$\|\mu_e-{}_{*}\omega_e\|_\infty$, which is lower bounded by $ \Omega(4^{-k})\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)$. Therefore
	\begin{equation}\label{e:nondefective.merge.entropy.disc.lbd}
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\tilde{\mu})
	\ge
	\frac{1}{2^{6k}}\Bigg(
	\max_{e\in J_a}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)
	\Bigg)^2\,.\end{equation}
We next turn to bounding the clause weights.
Recall that we use $q$ to denote the \textsc{bp} messages for the $\Lambda$-weighted model.
 Similarly as in
\eqref{e:log.gamma.crude.bound.eps} and \eqref{e:log.gamma.crude.bound.delta},
a crude application of
Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights} gives
	\[
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	\|\ln\gamma_e\|_\infty
	\le k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	\bigg(
	\delta_e
	+\dot{\delta}_e
	+\ddot{\delta}_e
	+{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\delta_e
	+{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\dot{\delta}_e
	\bigg)
	\]
where $\vec{\delta}=(\delta_e,\dot{\delta}_e,\ddot{\delta}_e,{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\delta_e,{}^\textup{m}\hspace{-2pt}\dot{\delta}_e)=\dddot{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize ERR}}}({}_{*}\dot{q}_e,\dot{q}_e)$. In order to bound $\vec{\delta}$ we can argue as follows. Let 
$\hat{p}_e \cong {}_{*}\omega_e/\dot{q}_e$ for all $e\in\delta a$, and let $Z_a=(\zeta_e)_{e\in\delta a}$ be the reweighting of $a$ such that
$\hat{p}_{au} = \textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{au}[(\dot{q}_{u'a}:u'\in\partial a\setminus u) ; Z_a]$ for all $u\in\partial a$. It follows
by another crude application of
Proposition~\ref{p:pair.clause.weights} that,
similarly as in 
\eqref{e:log.gamma.crude.bound.eps},
	\[
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	\|\ln\zeta_e\|_\infty
	\le k^{O(1)}
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	(\varepsilon_e
	+\dot{\varepsilon}_e+\ddot{\varepsilon}_e
	)
	\]
where 
$\vec{\varepsilon}=(\varepsilon_e,\dot{\varepsilon}_e,\ddot{\varepsilon}_e)
=\widehat{\textup{\textsf{\footnotesize ERR}}}(\hat{p}_e,\hat{q}_e)$. We can bound $\vec{\epsilon}$ by noting that 
	\[
	\frac{\hat{p}_e}{\hat{q}_e}
	\cong \frac{{}_{*}\omega_e/\dot{q}_e}{\mu_e/\dot{q}_e}
	\cong \frac{{}_{*}\omega_e}{\mu_e}
	= 1 + O\bigg( \frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)}{\vartheta_*}\bigg)\,,
	\]
and substituting into the previous expression gives the bound
	\[
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	\|\ln\zeta_e\|_\infty
	\le k^{O(1)}
	 \frac{\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)}{\vartheta_*}\,.
	\]
From the definition of $Z_a$, for each $u\in\partial a$ we have
	\[
	{}_{*}\omega_{au}
	\cong \dot{q}_{ua} \hat{p}_{au}
	= \dot{q}_{ua} 
	\textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{au}\bigg[
	\Big(\dot{q}_{u'a}:u'\in\partial a\setminus u
		\Big) ; Z_a\bigg]
	\cong
	g_{ua} \textup{\footnotesize\textsf{BP}}_{au}\bigg[ \Big(g_{u'a}
		:u'\in\partial a\setminus u\Big)\bigg]\,,
	\]
where $g_e$ is the probability measure on $\set{\SSPIN{r},\SSPIN{y},\SSPIN{g},\SSPIN{b}}^2$ such that $g_e \cong \zeta_e\dot{q}_e$. It follows that the measure
	\begin{equation}\label{e:nu.g.pair.model}
	\hat{\nu}_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\cong
	\hat{\varphi}_a(\smash{\vec{\sigma}}_{\delta a})
	\prod_{e\in\delta a} g_e(\sigma_e)
	\end{equation}
has marginal ${}_{*}\omega_e$ on each $e\in\delta a$. This can only occur if $g_e={}_{*}\dot{q}_e$ for all $e\in\delta a$ --- this is because $(\ln g_e)_{e\in\delta a}$ must be the Lagrange multipliers for the constrained optimization problem
	\[
	\max \Bigg\{
	\mathcal{H}(\hat{\nu}):
	\textup{$\hat{\nu}$ has marginals ${}_{*}\omega_e$}
	\Bigg\}\,,
	\]
and the Lagrange multipliers are unique so we must have $g_e={}_{*}\dot{q}_e$ for all $e\in\delta a$. It follows that 
	\[\zeta_e\cong 
	\frac{g_e}{\dot{q}_e}
	\cong \frac{{}_{*}\dot{q}_e }{\dot{q}_e}
	\,,\] so the above bound on $\|\ln\zeta\|_\infty$ implies a bound on $\vec{\delta}$. Altogether we obtain 
	\[
	\sum_{e\in\delta a}
	\|\ln\zeta_e\|_\infty
	\le \frac{k^{O(1)}}{\vartheta_*}
	\sum_{e\in\delta a} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\,.
	\]
Substituting into the definition of $\mathcal{A}$ from
\eqref{e:nondefective.merge.A.B} gives
	\[
	\mathcal{A}
	\le 
	\frac{k^{O(1)}}{(\vartheta_*)^2}
	\bigg(\sum_{e\in\delta a} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\bigg)^2
	\le
	\frac{k^{O(1)}}{(\vartheta_*)^2}
	\xi_a(J;\mu_{\delta'J})^2\,,
	\]
where the last step follows by Proposition~\ref{p:nice.tree.lagrange}.
Combining with \eqref{e:contradiction.a1} gives
	\[
	\mathcal{A}
	\le
	\frac{k^{O(1)}}{(\vartheta_*)^2}
		\xi_a(J;\mu_{\delta'J})^2
	\le
	\frac{k^{O(1)}(k^4 (\vartheta_*)^{1/4})^{2\mathcal{M}}}{(\vartheta_*)^2} 
	\Bigg(
		\max_{e\in\delta J_a} 	
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\Bigg)^2\,.
	\]
Next, very similarly to the derivation of 
\eqref{e:apply.cor.clause.weights}, for $b\in A_a$ and $e\in\delta b$ we have
	\[
	\|\ln\gamma_e\|_\infty
	\le 2^k \sum_{e\in\delta b
		\cap \delta J_a} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\,.
	\]
(Indeed, a clause $b\in A_a$ takes in the canonical product message ${}_{*}\dot{q}$ from above, and takes from below the messages $\dot{q}_e$ from the $\Lambda_{T_e}$-weighted measures (which are precisely the measures $\nu(e)$. Thus $\|\ln\gamma_e\|_\infty$ can be bounded in terms of the errors between $\dot{q}_e$ and ${}_{*}\dot{q}_e$ for $e\in\delta b\cap \delta J_a$. These in turn can be bounded in terms of the marginal discrepancies $\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))$, similarly as in
\eqref{e:apply.cor.clause.weights}.) Substituting into the definition of $\mathcal{B}$ from \eqref{e:nondefective.merge.A.B} gives
	\begin{align*}
	\mathcal{B}
	&\le
	2^{2k}
	\sum_{b\in A_a}
	\Bigg(
	\sum_{e\in \delta b \cap \delta J_a}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\Bigg)
	\Bigg(\sum_{e\in\delta b}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu) 
	+
	\sum_{e\in \delta b \cap \delta J_a}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))\Bigg)\\
	&\stackrel{\eqref{e:contradiction.a2}}{\le}
	2^{2k} 2k
	\Bigg(
	\max_{e'\in J_a} \textup{\textsf{disc}}_{e'}(\mu)
	\Bigg)
	\sum_{e\in\delta J_a}
	\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\nu(e))
	\stackrel{\eqref{e:contradiction.a2}}{\le}
	2^{2k} 2k
	\bigg( \frac{k^4}{2^k}\bigg)^{\mathcal{M}}
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in J_a}
		\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\Bigg)^2
	\,.
	\end{align*}
Substituting these bounds back into \eqref{e:nondefective.merge.A.B} and combining with
\eqref{e:nondefective.merge.entropy.disc.lbd} gives
	\[
	\frac1{2^{6k}}
	\bigg(\max_{e\in J_a}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\bigg)^2
	\le
	\mathcal{H}(\mu\,|\,\tilde{\mu} )
	\le \mathcal{A}+\mathcal{B}
	\le (\vartheta_*)^{\mathcal{M}/3}
	\Bigg(\max_{e\in J_a}\textup{\textsf{disc}}_e(\mu)\Bigg)^2\,.
	\]
Again recall from Definition~\ref{d:contained} that $\vartheta_*\equiv 2^{-k\zeta/6}$. Thus, as long as we have
	\begin{equation}\label{e:weaker.lbd.KAPPA}
	\kappa_*
	=\mathcal{M}^4 > \bigg(\frac{108}{\zeta}\bigg)^4\,,
	\end{equation}
we obtain the required contradiction.

