If we rematch the edges within $E_A$, and
also rematch the edges within $E_B$ (uniformly at random), the resulting $(\mathscr{G}',\smash{\vec{\sigma}}')$ will also be distributed roughly according to $\mathbf{P}$. The number of $B_1$-edges in $(\mathscr{G}',\smash{\vec{\sigma}}')$
is lower bounded by the number of switched edges from $E_A(\mathscr{G},\smash{\vec{\sigma}})$, so we conclude
	\[
	\frac{p(B_1)}{1-o_n(1)}
	\ge
	\frac{|E_A(\mathscr{G},\smash{\vec{\sigma}})|}{|E(\bm{L},j)|}
	\frac{p(A_1) p(A_2)}{p(A_1)+p(A_2)}
	\ge
	\Big\{1-o_n(1)\Big\}
	p(A_1) p(A_2)\,.
	\]
Recalling the definition of the $A_i,B_i$, the above can be rewritten as
	\[\omega_{\bm{L},j}(\SSPIN{r}\SSPIN{b})
	\ge \frac{\omega_{\bm{L},j}(\SSPIN{b}\SSPIN{b})
		\omega_{\bm{L},j}(\SSPIN{r}\SSPIN{y})}{1+o_k(1)}
	\ge \frac{\pi_v(\SPIN{+}\SPIN{+})
	{}_\star\hspace{-1pt}\pi_e(\SSPIN{r})}{11}\,,
